PROGRESSIVE_THEORY_DICT = {('EM', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: The Wi-Fi Login Password (What is a Proposition?)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          When you enter a Wi-Fi password on your phone, the router checks only one thing: Is the password Correct (True) or Incorrect (False)? There is no \'maybe\' or \'half-correct\'. In computer science, any statement that is strictly True or False is called a Proposition. Sentences like \'What is your name?\' (a question) or \'Please give me water\' (a request) are NOT propositions because they cannot be true or false.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: Dad\'s Promise (How Implication P → Q Really Works)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Imagine your father makes a promise: \'If you score AIR 1 in GATE (P), I will buy you a new bike (Q)\'. When would you call your dad a liar? ONLY if you score AIR 1 and he refuses to buy the bike (True → False = False). What if you did NOT score AIR 1? He never broke his promise, whether he buys the bike anyway or not! That is why whenever the condition P is False, the statement (P → Q) is ALWAYS considered TRUE in logic. This is known as Vacuous Truth.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Social Media Friends (Types of Relations Made Simple)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Think of people on Facebook or Instagram:\n• Reflexive: Can you follow or see your own profile? Yes! (Every person relates to themselves).\n• Symmetric: On Facebook, if Alice is friends with Bob, Bob is automatically friends with Alice (Friendship works both ways).\n• Transitive: If Alice trusts Bob, and Bob trusts Charlie, does Alice automatically trust Charlie? If yes, it is Transitive.\nWhen a relation is Reflexive + Symmetric + Transitive, it divides everyone into tight-knit friend groups called Equivalence Classes.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>1.1 Propositions & Basic Logic Connectives</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          A proposition is any clear statement that is either True (T) or False (F).<br/>• <b>NOT (~P):</b> Flips the truth. If P is True, ~P is False.<br/>• <b>AND (P ∧ Q):</b> True ONLY if BOTH P and Q are True (like both keys needed to open a safe).<br/>• <b>OR (P ∨ Q):</b> True if AT LEAST ONE of P or Q is True (False only if both are False).<br/>• <b>Implication (P → Q):</b> Means \'If P then Q\'. It is equivalent to <b>(~P ∨ Q)</b>.<br/>• <b>Double Arrow (P ↔ Q):</b> True when both have the exact same value (both True or both False).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: P → Q is False ONLY when P is True and Q is False. In all other 3 cases, it is ALWAYS True!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>1.2 Contrapositive, Converse, and Inverse</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          For any statement \'If P then Q\' (P → Q):<br/>• <b>Contrapositive (~Q → ~P):</b> \'If NOT Q, then NOT P\'. This is <b>100% equivalent</b> to the original statement! (Always use this in GATE when solving proofs).<br/>• <b>Converse (Q → P):</b> \'If Q, then P\'. NOT always equal to original.<br/>• <b>Inverse (~P → ~Q):</b> \'If NOT P, then NOT Q\'. Equal to Converse, but NOT the original.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: A statement and its Contrapositive always have the exact same truth value: (P → Q) ≡ (~Q → ~P).\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>1.3 Quantifiers: For All (∀) vs There Exists (∃)</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Universal Quantifier (∀x):</b> Means \'For EVERY single item in the group\'. Even if one item fails, the whole statement becomes False.<br/>• <b>Existential Quantifier (∃x):</b> Means \'There is AT LEAST ONE item\'. As long as one item works, it is True.<br/>• <b>Negation Rule (De Morgan):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;~[ For all x, P(x) ] = There exists at least one x where NOT P(x).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;~[ There exists x, P(x) ] = For all x, NOT P(x).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Negating \'All birds can fly\' is NOT \'No birds can fly\'. It is simply \'There exists at least one bird that cannot fly\' (like a penguin)!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>1.4 Counting Relations on a Set of Size n</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          If a set A has n elements, total pairs in A × A is n².<br/>• <b>Total possible relations:</b> 2^(n²)<br/>• <b>Reflexive relations:</b> 2^(n² - n)<br/>• <b>Symmetric relations:</b> 2^(n(n+1)/2)<br/>• <b>Reflexive + Symmetric:</b> 2^(n(n-1)/2)<br/>• <b>Equivalence relations:</b> Counted by Bell Numbers: B(1)=1, B(2)=2, B(3)=5, B(4)=15.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Remember the Bell number for size 4: B(4) = 15. GATE asks this number directly!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Counting Symmetric Relations\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> Set A has 4 elements: A = {1, 2, 3, 4}. How many symmetric relations can be formed on set A?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Note the formula for symmetric relations: Total = 2^(n(n+1)/2).<br/>• Step 2: Here n = 4.<br/>• Step 3: Calculate the power: (4 × (4 + 1)) / 2 = (4 × 5) / 2 = 20 / 2 = 10.<br/>• Step 4: Compute 2^10 = 1024.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Total Symmetric Relations = 1024.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: The Empty Set (Vacuous Truth)</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          If a question says \'For all elements in set S, property P holds\', and set S is completely EMPTY, the statement is ALWAYS TRUE! Don\'t mark it False.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('EM', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: Google Maps Road Network (Vertices and Edges)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Think of Google Maps: every city or junction is a dot (Vertex), and every road connecting two junctions is a line (Edge). The degree of a junction is simply the number of roads meeting at that point. If you add up the number of road-ends across all junctions, every road has 2 ends, so the sum of all degrees is always twice the number of roads. This simple fact is called the Handshaking Lemma.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: The Cheapest Metro Track (What is a Tree?)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Suppose you need to connect 6 metro stations so passengers can travel between any two stations without wasting money building redundant loops. The minimum number of track segments needed is always (Stations - 1) = 6 - 1 = 5 tracks. A connected network with no circular loops is called a Tree. If you add even 1 extra track, you create a loop; if you remove 1, someone gets disconnected.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Printed Circuit Boards (Planar Graphs)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          When engineers print electrical copper wires on a flat board, wires cannot cross each other or they will cause a short circuit. A graph that can be drawn on a flat sheet without any edges crossing is called a Planar Graph. Euler proved that for any such flat network: (Vertices - Edges + Enclosed Regions = 2).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>2.1 Handshaking Lemma & Degree Sum Rule</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          In any undirected graph:<br/>• <b>Sum of degrees of all vertices = 2 × (Number of Edges)</b>.<br/>• <b>Direct result:</b> The count of vertices having an ODD degree is ALWAYS EVEN (0, 2, 4, 6...).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: If an exam question gives vertex degrees like [3, 3, 3, 2], degree sum = 11 (odd number), which is IMPOSSIBLE! No such graph can ever exist.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>2.2 Trees & Spanning Trees</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          A tree is a connected graph with no cycles.<br/>• A tree with n vertices always has exactly <b>n - 1 edges</b>.<br/>• There is exactly one simple path between any two vertices.<br/>• <b>Cayley\'s Formula:</b> Number of labeled spanning trees for complete graph Kn is <b>n^(n - 2)</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: For n = 4 vertices: Spanning trees = 4^(4-2) = 4^2 = 16. For n = 5: 5^(5-2) = 5^3 = 125.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>2.3 Bipartite Graphs & Graph Coloring</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Bipartite Graph:</b> You can divide vertices into two sets (Boys and Girls) such that edges only go between sets, never within the same set.<br/>• <b>Check rule:</b> A graph is Bipartite if and only if it has <b>NO ODD-LENGTH CYCLES</b> (no triangles, no 5-cycles).<br/>• <b>Chromatic Number χ(G):</b> Minimum colors needed so no two connected dots share the same color. Trees need 2 colors. Bipartite graphs need 2 colors.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: If a graph has even one cycle of odd length (like 3 or 5), it CANNOT be bipartite!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>2.4 Planar Graphs & Euler\'s Formula</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • For any connected flat (planar) graph: <b>V - E + R = 2</b> (where R includes the outer space).<br/>• <b>Edge limit:</b> For simple planar graphs with 3 or more vertices: <b>E ≤ 3V - 6</b>.<br/>• If graph has no triangles (like bipartite): <b>E ≤ 2V - 4</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: K5 (5 vertices, 10 edges) is NOT planar because 10 > 3(5) - 6 = 9. K3,3 is also NOT planar.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Finding Regions in a Planar Graph\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> A connected planar graph has 10 vertices and 15 edges. How many regions does it divide the plane into?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Write Euler\'s formula: V - E + R = 2.<br/>• Step 2: Plug in V = 10 and E = 15: 10 - 15 + R = 2.<br/>• Step 3: Simplify: -5 + R = 2.<br/>• Step 4: Solve for R: R = 2 + 5 = 7 regions.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Total Regions R = 7 (6 inside faces + 1 outside face).\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Interior vs Total Regions</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Euler\'s R = 7 includes the 1 outer infinite region! If the question asks for \'enclosed\' or \'bounded\' regions, subtract 1: Bounded = 7 - 1 = 6.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('EM', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: Resizing a Photo on Your Phone (Determinant)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          When you pinch and zoom a photo on your smartphone, the graphics chip multiplies coordinates by a 2x2 matrix. The Determinant tells you how much the picture\'s area changes. If det = 2, the image area doubles. If det = 1, it only rotates without stretching. If det = 0, the photo is completely squished flat into a single line (area becomes 0), meaning you can never restore the original picture (it has no inverse!).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: The Google PageRank Magic (Eigenvalues & Eigenvectors)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          When you search on Google, how does it know which page is most important? Google models billions of web pages as an enormous matrix of links. An Eigenvector is a special list of numbers that doesn\'t change direction when multiplied by the link matrix—it represents the steady popularity score of every website. The matching Eigenvalue is 1.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Solving 10,000 Equations in Seconds (LU Decomposition)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          If you have a set of equations Ax = b, solving them from scratch every time takes a lot of computing power (O(n³)). LU Decomposition splits matrix A once into a Lower triangle L and an Upper triangle U. After this one-time split, solving for any new target \'b\' takes just a split second (O(n²))!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>3.1 Rank of a Matrix & Solving AX = B</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          Rank is simply the number of non-zero rows after converting the matrix to row echelon form.<br/>For system AX = B with n unknowns:<br/>• <b>Unique Solution:</b> Rank(A) = Rank([A|B]) = n.<br/>• <b>Infinitely Many Solutions:</b> Rank(A) = Rank([A|B]) < n.<br/>• <b>No Solution (Inconsistent):</b> Rank(A) < Rank([A|B]).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: For homogeneous equations AX = 0, a non-zero solution exists IF AND ONLY IF det(A) = 0!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>3.2 Eigenvalues & Their Super Useful Shortcuts</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          Eigenvalues (λ) are found using det(A - λI) = 0.<br/>• <b>Trace Shortcut:</b> Sum of all eigenvalues = Sum of diagonal elements (Trace of A).<br/>• <b>Determinant Shortcut:</b> Product of all eigenvalues = det(A).<br/>• If A has eigenvalues λ1, λ2, then:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• A² has eigenvalues λ1², λ2².<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• A⁻¹ has eigenvalues 1/λ1, 1/λ2 (if det ≠ 0).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• A + 3I has eigenvalues λ1 + 3, λ2 + 3.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: In GATE, use the Trace and Determinant shortcuts first! You can eliminate 3 out of 4 options in 15 seconds without solving the characteristic equation.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>3.3 LU Decomposition Basics</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          Matrix A is factored into A = L × U (L = Lower triangular, U = Upper triangular).<br/>• In Doolittle\'s method, diagonal of L has all 1s.<br/>• Because det(L) = 1, <b>det(A) is simply the product of the diagonal elements of U</b>!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: det(A) = det(L) × det(U). In Doolittle, det(A) = u11 × u22 × ... × unn.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Finding Eigenvalues Quickly\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> Matrix A = [[3, 2], [1, 4]]. Find its eigenvalues without writing full determinant expansion.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Calculate Trace (sum of diagonal): 3 + 4 = 7.<br/>• Step 2: Calculate Determinant: (3 × 4) - (2 × 1) = 12 - 2 = 10.<br/>• Step 3: We need two numbers whose SUM = 7 and PRODUCT = 10.<br/>• Step 4: The numbers are clearly 2 and 5 (since 2 + 5 = 7 and 2 × 5 = 10).\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Eigenvalues are λ = 2 and λ = 5.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Zero as an Eigenvalue</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          If even one eigenvalue is 0, then the product of eigenvalues is 0, which means det(A) = 0! Such a matrix has NO inverse.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('EM', 3): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: The False Alarm Medical Test (Bayes\' Theorem)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Suppose a disease is very rare (only 1 in 1,000 people have it). A medical test is 99% accurate. If you test positive, are you 99% likely to be sick? NO! In a town of 1,000 people: 1 sick person tests positive, but 10 healthy people will also test positive by mistake (1% false alarm). So out of 11 positive results, only 1 is actually sick (about 9% chance)! Bayes\' Theorem calculates your true probability by combining the test result with how common the disease actually is.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: Waiting for the Elevator (Poisson vs Exponential)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          How many people enter an elevator per hour? That is a count of discrete events: Poisson Distribution. How many minutes do you wait between two elevator arrivals? That is continuous time: Exponential Distribution. The exponential distribution is \'memoryless\'—even if you already waited 5 minutes, your chance of waiting 2 more minutes is the exact same as when you first arrived!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: The Crest of a Rollercoaster (Maxima and Minima)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          At the very top peak of a rollercoaster track, the car stops rising for a split second: vertical speed is zero (First derivative f\'(x) = 0). As it tips downward, the curve bends down like an upside-down bowl (Second derivative f\'\'(x) < 0). That is the mathematical test for a local maximum!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>4.1 Limits, L\'Hôpital\'s Rule & Maxima/Minima</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>L\'Hôpital\'s Rule:</b> When a limit gives 0/0 or ∞/∞, take derivative of top and derivative of bottom: lim f(x)/g(x) = lim f\'(x)/g\'(x).<br/>• <b>Finding Maxima/Minima:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Set f\'(x) = 0 to find critical points.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Check f\'\'(x): if negative (< 0) -> Maximum; if positive (> 0) -> Minimum.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Apply L\'Hôpital ONLY when the fraction evaluates to 0/0 or ∞/∞. Don\'t apply it if the denominator is a normal non-zero number!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>4.2 Conditional Probability & Bayes\' Formula</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>P(A|B):</b> Probability of A happening given that B already happened = P(A ∩ B) / P(B).<br/>• <b>Independent Events:</b> P(A ∩ B) = P(A) × P(B).<br/>• <b>Bayes\' Theorem:</b> P(Cause A | Effect B) = [ P(B|A) × P(A) ] / P(B).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Two events with positive probabilities CANNOT be both mutually exclusive (cannot happen together) and independent at the same time!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>4.3 Standard Probability Distributions to Memorize</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Poisson Distribution:</b> Mean = λ, <b>Variance = λ</b> (Mean equals Variance!).<br/>• <b>Uniform Distribution U(a, b):</b> Mean = (a + b)/2, <b>Variance = (b - a)² / 12</b>.<br/>• <b>Exponential Distribution:</b> Mean = 1/λ, Variance = 1/λ².\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: If an exam question mentions a distribution where Mean = 3 and Variance = 3, it is definitely a POISSON distribution with λ = 3.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Bayes\' Theorem in Action\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> Box 1 has 2 Gold coins. Box 2 has 1 Gold and 1 Silver coin. You pick a box at random and draw a Gold coin. What is the probability it came from Box 1?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Probability of picking either box = 1/2.<br/>• Step 2: P(Gold | Box 1) = 2/2 = 1. P(Gold | Box 2) = 1/2.<br/>• Step 3: Total probability of Gold: (1/2 × 1) + (1/2 × 1/2) = 1/2 + 1/4 = 3/4.<br/>• Step 4: By Bayes\' Theorem: P(Box 1 | Gold) = (1/2 × 1) / (3/4) = (1/2) / (3/4) = 2/3.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Probability = 2/3 (or 66.7%).\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Uniform Distribution Variance Formula</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          The denominator in Uniform variance is 12: Var = (b - a)² / 12. Many students wrongly write 4 or 6. Don\'t lose 1 mark here!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('DL', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: The Smart Home Light Switch (Boolean Logic)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Imagine a hallway light with two switches (one downstairs, one upstairs). The light turns on when either switch is flipped, but turns off if both are in the same position. That is an XOR gate! Boolean algebra is the math of simple on/off switches that makes computers possible.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: Cleaning Up Redundant Wire Clutter (Karnaugh Maps)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Imagine you built a circuit with 15 logic gates that takes 5 seconds to respond. A Karnaugh Map (K-Map) is a visual puzzle grid that groups adjacent 1s into groups of 2, 4, or 8. By circling these blocks, you can reduce those 15 messy gates into just 2 simple gates, making the chip 10x faster and cheaper to manufacture.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: The Car Gear Shift (Don\'t Care Conditions)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          In a car with 5 gears (1 to 5), an electronic 3-bit sensor represents gears: 001 is 1st gear, 010 is 2nd, etc. But binary codes 110 (6) and 111 (7) can never physically happen because the gearbox doesn\'t have 6th or 7th gears. We mark these unused codes as \'Don\'t Care\' (X). On a K-Map, we can treat X as 1 if it helps make a bigger group, or treat it as 0 if it doesn\'t!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>5.1 Boolean Laws & De Morgan\'s Rules</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>De Morgan\'s Laws:</b> (A + B)\' = A\' · B\' &nbsp;|&nbsp; (A · B)\' = A\' + B\' (Break the line, change the sign!).<br/>• <b>Consensus Theorem:</b> AB + A\'C + BC = AB + A\'C (The term BC is redundant and can be dropped).<br/>• <b>Duality Principle:</b> Swap AND with OR, and swap 0 with 1. (Keep variables as they are).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: NAND and NOR are called UNIVERSAL GATES because you can build ANY digital circuit using only NAND or only NOR gates!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>5.2 K-Map Rules & Essential Prime Implicants</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • K-Map cells are arranged in <b>Gray Code order</b> (00, 01, 11, 10) so only 1 bit changes between adjacent cells.<br/>• <b>Prime Implicant (PI):</b> The largest possible rectangle of 1s (size 1, 2, 4, 8, 16).<br/>• <b>Essential Prime Implicant (EPI):</b> A prime implicant that covers at least one \'1\' that NO OTHER group covers.<br/>• Minimal SOP = All EPIs + minimal other PIs to cover any remaining 1s.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Always group in powers of two (1, 2, 4, 8, 16). Larger groups mean fewer variables in the final answer!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>5.3 Static-1 Hazards & How to Fix Them</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          A Static-1 hazard happens when an output is supposed to stay 1, but momentarily glitches to 0 because an inverter gate takes a few nanoseconds of delay.<br/>• <b>Fix:</b> Look at the K-Map. If two adjacent 1s belong to two different groups, add a redundant overlapping circle (consensus term) to bridge them.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Two-level AND-OR (SOP) circuits can only have Static-1 hazards. They NEVER have Static-0 hazards!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Simplifying with Consensus Theorem\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> Simplify the Boolean expression: F = A B + A\' C + B C.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Check the 3 terms: AB, A\'C, BC.<br/>• Step 2: Notice that variable A appears normal in first term (A) and complemented in second term (A\').<br/>• Step 3: The third term (BC) contains the remaining variables from the first two terms.<br/>• Step 4: By Consensus Theorem, term BC is completely redundant and can be dropped!\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Simplified Expression = A B + A\' C.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Gray Code Ordering in K-Maps</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Never write columns as 00, 01, 10, 11! The correct Gray code is 00, 01, 11, 10. Swapping 11 and 10 will ruin your entire K-Map grouping.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('DL', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: The TV Remote Input Selector (Multiplexer 2^n to 1)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Your TV has multiple inputs: Cable TV, PlayStation, Xbox, and Chromecast. But the TV screen can only show 1 video at a time. The remote control uses select buttons to choose which input feeds to the screen. A Multiplexer (MUX) does the exact same thing: it selects 1 of many data inputs and forwards it to a single output line based on select lines.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: The Memory Address Postal Worker (Decoder n to 2^n)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          When the CPU wants to read from RAM memory slot #5, it sends binary 101 (3 bits). A 3-to-8 Decoder reads 101 and lights up exactly wire #5, leaving all other 7 memory wires off. A decoder turns a compressed binary code into a single active line.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Fast Addition (Carry Lookahead Adder)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          In regular addition, the 4th digit cannot be added until the carry bit ripples from digit 1 to 2 to 3 (Ripple Carry Adder: slow like dominoes). A Carry Lookahead Adder (CLA) uses clever logic to calculate all carry bits simultaneously in just 2 gate delays, making 64-bit addition blazing fast.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>6.1 Multiplexers (MUX) as Universal Function Generators</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          A 2^n-to-1 MUX has 2^n input lines, n select lines, and 1 output.<br/>• <b>2-to-1 MUX equation:</b> Y = S\' I0 + S I1.<br/>• <b>4-to-1 MUX equation:</b> Y = S1\' S0\' I0 + S1\' S0 I1 + S1 S0\' I2 + S1 S0 I3.<br/>• <b>Universal property:</b> ANY n-variable Boolean function can be implemented using a 2^(n-1)-to-1 MUX without needing extra gates!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Total 2-to-1 MUXes needed to build an N-to-1 MUX is always exactly N - 1. Example: To build a 16-to-1 MUX requires 15 2-to-1 MUXes.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>6.2 Decoders & Priority Encoders</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Decoder (n to 2^n):</b> Generates all 2^n minterms. If active-high, output is AND; if active-low, output is NAND.<br/>• <b>Priority Encoder:</b> If multiple inputs are active at the same time, it outputs the code of only the highest priority input.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Always check if the Enable pin (E) is Active-Low (E\'). If E\' = 1, the decoder is OFF and all outputs remain 0!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>6.3 Half Adder vs Full Adder</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Half Adder (adds 2 bits):</b> Sum = A ⊕ B, Carry = A · B. (Needs 1 XOR, 1 AND).<br/>• <b>Full Adder (adds 3 bits):</b> Sum = A ⊕ B ⊕ Cin, Carry = AB + Cin(A ⊕ B).<br/>• Can build 1 Full Adder using <b>2 Half Adders and 1 OR gate</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Minimum NAND gates needed: 5 NAND gates for a Half Adder, and 9 NAND gates for a Full Adder.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Building 64-to-1 MUX Using 4-to-1 MUXes\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> How many 4-to-1 MUXes are needed to build a single 64-to-1 MUX?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: First level: 64 inputs / 4 = 16 MUXes (produces 16 outputs).<br/>• Step 2: Second level: 16 inputs / 4 = 4 MUXes (produces 4 outputs).<br/>• Step 3: Third level: 4 inputs / 4 = 1 MUX (produces final 1 output).<br/>• Step 4: Sum all levels: 16 + 4 + 1 = 21 MUXes.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Total 4-to-1 MUXes required = 21.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Missing Enable Lines</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          When cascading decoders (e.g. building a 4-to-16 decoder using 2-to-4 decoders), use the highest address bit to drive the Enable pin of the decoders!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('DL', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: The Push-Button Lamp (Toggle Flip-Flop)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          A bedside lamp with a single push button: Press it once -> light turns ON. Press it again -> light turns OFF. The button remembers its previous state! This 1-bit memory is a Toggle (T) Flip-Flop. Unlike combinational circuits whose output depends only on current inputs, sequential circuits have MEMORY.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: The Car Odometer (Asynchronous vs Synchronous Counter)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          In an old car odometer, when the 1s wheel rolls from 9 to 0, it pushes the 10s wheel. That is an Asynchronous (Ripple) counter: each digit waits for the previous one to finish moving. In modern digital cars, all digits update at the exact same clock pulse: a Synchronous Counter.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: The Subway Turnstile (Finite State Machine)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          A subway turnstile has two states: Locked and Unlocked. Tapping your metro card moves it to Unlocked. Pushing through the turnstile moves it back to Locked. This is a Finite State Machine: it remembers which state it is in and changes state only when valid triggers happen.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>7.1 The 4 Basic Flip-Flops & Their Equations</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>SR Flip-Flop:</b> Q(next) = S + R\' Q (Condition: S·R = 0, S=1 and R=1 is invalid).<br/>• <b>JK Flip-Flop:</b> Q(next) = J Q\' + K\' Q (Toggles when J=1, K=1; fixes the SR bug!).<br/>• <b>D Flip-Flop:</b> Q(next) = D (Delays/Stores whatever data is at input D).<br/>• <b>T Flip-Flop:</b> Q(next) = T ⊕ Q (Inverts state when T=1; keeps state when T=0).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Quick Characteristic Equations to memorize:<br/>• JK: Q+ = JQ\' + K\'Q<br/>• D: Q+ = D<br/>• T: Q+ = T ⊕ Q.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>7.2 Setup Time, Hold Time & Maximum Clock Frequency</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Setup Time (t_setup):</b> Data must be stable BEFORE the clock tick.<br/>• <b>Hold Time (t_hold):</b> Data must remain stable AFTER the clock tick.<br/>• <b>Max Clock Frequency:</b> Clock Period T ≥ t_clk_to_q + t_combinational + t_setup. Frequency f_max = 1 / T.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Hold time violations CANNOT be fixed by slowing down the clock! They depend only on internal gate delays.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>7.3 Counters: Mod-N, Ring & Johnson Counters</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Mod-N Counter:</b> Counts N states (needs ⌈log2(N)⌉ flip-flops).<br/>• <b>Ring Counter:</b> N flip-flops give exactly <b>N states</b> (1 hot bit circulating).<br/>• <b>Johnson (Twisted Ring) Counter:</b> N flip-flops give <b>2N states</b> (Inverted output fed back).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: With 4 flip-flops: Standard counter gives 2^4 = 16 states, Johnson counter gives 2(4) = 8 states, Ring counter gives 4 states.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Maximum Clock Frequency\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> A circuit has flip-flop delay t_cq = 2 ns, combinational logic delay = 5 ns, and setup time = 3 ns. What is the maximum operating clock frequency?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Compute minimum clock period: T_min = t_cq + t_comb + t_setup.<br/>• Step 2: Add values: T_min = 2 ns + 5 ns + 3 ns = 10 ns = 10 × 10^-9 s.<br/>• Step 3: Frequency f_max = 1 / T_min = 1 / (10 × 10^-9) = 10^8 Hz = 100 MHz.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Maximum Clock Frequency = 100 MHz.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Race-Around Condition</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Race-around happens in level-triggered JK flip-flops when J=1, K=1 and clock pulse width is longer than flip-flop delay. Solution: Use Edge-Triggered or Master-Slave flip-flops.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('DL', 3): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: The Car Odometer Rolling from 99999 to 00000 (Two\'s Complement)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          When an odometer hits 99,999 and you drive 1 more km, it rolls over to 00,000. In computer math, if an 8-bit signed integer is at +127 (01111111) and you add 1, it wraps around to -128 (10000000)! This is Two\'s Complement arithmetic. Computers love it because subtraction is done by simply adding the negative number.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: The Rocket Disaster (Floating Point Overflow)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          In 1996, the Ariane 5 space rocket exploded 37 seconds after launch because the software converted a 64-bit floating point number into a 16-bit integer. The number was too big to fit into 16 bits, causing an overflow error that crashed the navigation engines. Understanding number limits is critical!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Scientific Notation on a Calculator (IEEE 754)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          On a pocket calculator, very big numbers are shown as 6.02 × 10^23. In computers, IEEE 754 does the exact same thing in binary: 1 Sign bit (+ or -), 8 Exponent bits (power of 2), and 23 Mantissa bits (the actual precision digits). This lets 32 bits represent both the width of an atom and the distance to Mars.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>8.1 Signed Number Ranges (n bits)</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Unsigned:</b> 0 to 2^n - 1.<br/>• <b>Sign-Magnitude:</b> -(2^(n-1) - 1) to +(2^(n-1) - 1). (Has two zeros: +0 and -0).<br/>• <b>1\'s Complement:</b> -(2^(n-1) - 1) to +(2^(n-1) - 1). (Also has two zeros).<br/>• <b>2\'s Complement:</b> <b>-2^(n-1) to +(2^(n-1) - 1)</b>. (Has only ONE unique zero! Has one extra negative number).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: For 8 bits in 2\'s complement: Range is -128 to +127. The binary code 10000000 represents -128.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>8.2 IEEE 754 Single Precision (32-bit)</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Bit Split:</b> 1 Sign bit (s) | 8 Exponent bits (e, Bias = 127) | 23 Mantissa bits (f).<br/>• <b>Formula:</b> Value = (-1)^s × (1.f) × 2^(e - 127).<br/>• Note: The leading \'1.\' is hidden (not stored in the 23 bits).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: If e = 0 and f = 0, it represents ±0. If e = 255 and f = 0, it represents ±Infinity. If e = 255 and f ≠ 0, it is NaN (Not a Number).\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>8.3 Overflow Detection Rule</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          In 2\'s complement addition:<br/>• Overflow happens when two positive numbers add to give a negative result, OR two negative numbers add to give a positive result.<br/>• <b>Hardware test:</b> Overflow = <b>Carry_in ⊕ Carry_out</b> of the sign bit (MSB).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Adding a positive and a negative number can NEVER produce an overflow!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Converting -9.75 to IEEE 754 Hex\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> Represent decimal number -9.75 in 32-bit IEEE 754 format.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Sign is negative ⇒ Sign bit s = 1.<br/>• Step 2: Convert 9.75 to binary: 9 = 1001, 0.75 = 0.5 + 0.25 = 0.11 ⇒ 1001.11.<br/>• Step 3: Normalize: 1001.11 = 1.00111 × 2^3.<br/>• Step 4: Biased exponent: E = 3 + 127 = 130 ⇒ in binary 10000010.<br/>• Step 5: Mantissa (drop leading 1): 00111000... (23 bits).<br/>• Step 6: Combine: [1] [10000010] [00111000000000000000000] = 0xC11C0000.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Hex representation = 0xC11C0000.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Single vs Double Precision Bias</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          For Single Precision (32-bit), Bias = 127. For Double Precision (64-bit with 11 exponent bits), Bias = 1023 (NOT 1024!).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('COA', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: The Scavenger Hunt Clues (Addressing Modes)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Imagine a scavenger hunt game:\n• Immediate: The clue card gives you ₹500 cash directly taped to it (data is inside the instruction!).\n• Direct: The card gives an address: \'Go to House #42\' (direct memory address).\n• Indirect: The card says: \'Open locker 10; inside locker 10 is a note with the real address\' (Pointer to pointer).\n• PC-Relative: The card says: \'From where you are right now, take 5 steps forward\' (used for loops and branch jumps).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: Little-Endian vs Big-Endian Mail (Byte Ordering)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          When writing an address, does the country come first or last? In Big-Endian, you write the Most Significant part first (Country, then City, then House number). In Little-Endian, you write the Least Significant byte first (House number first, then City, Country last). Intel x86 processors use Little-Endian; network packets use Big-Endian.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Expanding Menu Combos (Expanding Opcode)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          In a fast food shop, a 2-bit code lets you choose 4 combos. But what if you want 3 popular big meals (requiring lots of side dishes) and 8 small snacks (needing fewer sides)? By using one code as an \'escape code\' to unlock longer codes, you can support both. This is Expanding Opcode.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>9.1 Effective Address (EA) in Common Addressing Modes</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          The Effective Address (EA) is the exact RAM address where the data is stored.<br/>• <b>Immediate:</b> Operand is in instruction. EA = None. (0 memory accesses).<br/>• <b>Direct:</b> EA = Address field. (1 memory access).<br/>• <b>Indirect:</b> EA = M[Address field]. (2 memory accesses).<br/>• <b>Register:</b> Data is in CPU register. EA = None. (0 memory accesses, ultra-fast!).<br/>• <b>Register Indirect:</b> EA = [Register content]. (1 memory access).<br/>• <b>PC-Relative:</b> EA = PC + Offset. (Used for Branch/Jump instructions).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: In PC-Relative addressing, remember that PC has ALREADY been incremented to the next instruction before the offset is added: EA = Next_PC + Offset!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>9.2 Hardwired vs Microprogrammed Control Unit</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Hardwired:</b> Built using fast digital logic gates. Blazing fast (RISC processors), but impossible to modify once manufactured.<br/>• <b>Microprogrammed:</b> Control signals are stored in a small ROM (Control Memory). Slower (CISC processors), but flexible and easy to update with firmware.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Horizontal microprogramming has 1 bit per control signal (no decoder needed, wide micro-words). Vertical microprogramming encodes signals with decoders (narrow words, smaller memory).\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Expanding Opcode\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> A 16-bit computer uses 4-bit register addresses. It supports 3-register instructions and 2-register instructions. If there are 14 different 3-register instructions, how many 2-register instructions can be supported?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Total bits = 16. A 3-register instruction uses 3 × 4 = 12 bits for registers.<br/>• Step 2: Remaining bits for opcode = 16 - 12 = 4 bits (Total patterns = 2^4 = 16).<br/>• Step 3: 14 patterns are used for 3-register instructions. Unused patterns = 16 - 14 = 2.<br/>• Step 4: For 2-register instructions, only 2 × 4 = 8 bits are needed for registers. Opcode expands by 4 bits!<br/>• Step 5: Number of 2-register instructions = (Unused patterns) × 2^4 = 2 × 16 = 32.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Maximum 2-register instructions = 32.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Byte vs Word Addressable</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Always check if memory is byte-addressable (8 bits per address) or word-addressable (e.g. 32 bits = 4 bytes per address). A 64 MB memory needs 26 bits if byte-addressed, but only 24 bits if 4-byte-word addressed!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('COA', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: Multiplying by 99 in Your Head (Booth\'s Algorithm)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          If someone asks you to multiply 27 by 99, do you do 99 separate additions? No! You do 27 × (100 - 1) = 2700 - 27 = 2673 (just one subtraction and one shift!). Booth\'s Algorithm does the exact same trick in binary: when it sees a long run of 1s (like 0111110), it does 1 subtraction at the start and 1 addition at the end, skipping all the middle steps!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: The VIP Express Package Delivery (Direct Memory Access - DMA)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          When a 4K movie downloads from hard drive to RAM, if the CPU had to move every single byte one by one, the CPU would freeze. A DMA Controller steps in, takes control of the bus, and copies the data directly between disk and RAM while the CPU keeps running your games without interruption.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Phone Notification Interrupts (Interrupt-Driven I/O)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Instead of checking your phone screen every 2 seconds to see if you got a WhatsApp message (Polling / Programmed I/O: wastes battery and time!), your phone stays asleep until a notification sound rings (Interrupt). The CPU only pauses what it is doing when a real event occurs.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>10.1 Booth\'s Multiplication Rules (Q_i, Q_{i-1})</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          Scan bits from right to left with Q_{-1} initialized to 0:<br/>• <b>0 0:</b> Just Arithmetic Right Shift (ARS).<br/>• <b>0 1:</b> Add Multiplicand (A ← A + M), then ARS.<br/>• <b>1 0:</b> Subtract Multiplicand (A ← A - M), then ARS.<br/>• <b>1 1:</b> Just Arithmetic Right Shift (ARS).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Shift in Booth\'s is strictly ARITHMETIC Right Shift (replicates sign bit, preserving positive/negative sign)!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>10.2 DMA Cycle Stealing Percentage</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          In cycle stealing mode, DMA steals 1 memory bus cycle from the CPU whenever a data unit is ready.<br/>• <b>CPU Time Stolen (%):</b> (Device Transfer Rate / Memory Bus Bandwidth) × 100%.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Percentage of CPU time available for normal work = 100% - (Percentage stolen by DMA).\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: DMA Bus Overhead\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> A hard disk transfers data at 2 MB/sec. The memory bus has cycle time 200 ns and transfers 1 byte per cycle. In cycle-stealing mode, what percentage of CPU time is stolen by DMA?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Calculate Memory Bus Bandwidth: 1 Byte / (200 × 10^-9 s) = 5,000,000 Bytes/s = 5 MB/sec.<br/>• Step 2: Hard disk transfer rate = 2 MB/sec.<br/>• Step 3: Fraction of time stolen = 2 MB/s / 5 MB/s = 2/5 = 0.40.<br/>• Step 4: Percentage stolen = 40%.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 CPU time consumed by DMA = 40% (60% remaining for CPU).\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Vectored vs Non-Vectored Interrupts</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Vectored interrupts supply the memory address of the handler directly on the bus. Non-vectored interrupts have a fixed, hardwired address in the CPU.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('COA', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: The Kitchen Spice Rack (Cache Memory Locality)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          When cooking dinner, a chef keeps salt, pepper, and oil on a tiny shelf right above the stove (L1 Cache: instant 1-second access). They don\'t walk down to the cold basement pantry (Main RAM: slow 1-minute walk) every time they need a pinch of salt! Because you tend to reuse recent items (Temporal Locality) and nearby items (Spatial Locality), 95% of cooking needs are met from the spice rack.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: Assigned Parking Spots (Cache Mapping)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Direct Mapped: Your apartment number determines your exact parking slot (Slot = Flat % 10). If Flat 104 and 204 arrive, they fight over Slot 4 (Conflict Miss!).\n• Fully Associative: Park in ANY vacant slot anywhere in the lot (Zero conflicts, but you need a security guard to search the whole lot).\n• Set-Associative: Each floor gets a 4-car designated cluster (The best balance of speed and cost!).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Pencil Scratchpad vs Ink Ledger (Write-Through vs Write-Back)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Write-Through updates both cache and main memory immediately on every write (slow, but safe). Write-Back writes only to the cache, marking it with a \'Dirty Bit\'. It only copies the dirty block to main RAM when that block is being kicked out (much faster!).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>11.1 Address Split in Cache Mapping</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          For address length P bits, Block Size B = 2^b bytes (Offset = b bits):<br/>• <b>Direct Mapped:</b> [ Tag | Line Index (c bits) | Block Offset (b bits) ]. Tag = P - c - b.<br/>• <b>k-Way Set Associative:</b> [ Tag | Set Index (s bits) | Block Offset (b bits) ].<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Number of Sets S = (Total Lines) / k = 2^s.<br/>• <b>Fully Associative:</b> [ Tag | Block Offset (b bits) ]. (No index bits!).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Always divide Total Cache Lines by k to find the number of sets: Sets = Lines / k. Never forget this step!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>11.2 Average Memory Access Time (AMAT)</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Formula:</b> AMAT = Hit_Time + (Miss_Rate × Miss_Penalty).<br/>• <b>Two-Level Cache:</b> AMAT = t_L1 + (Miss_Rate_L1 × [ t_L2 + (Miss_Rate_L2_local × t_main) ]).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: The 3 C\'s of Cache Misses: Compulsory (first-time access), Capacity (cache is too small), Conflict (multiple blocks fight for the same set).\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Set-Associative Cache Bits\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> A 32-bit processor has a 64 KB 4-way set-associative cache with 32-byte blocks. Find: (1) Offset bits, (2) Set index bits, (3) Tag bits.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Block Size = 32 Bytes = 2^5 Bytes ⇒ Offset b = 5 bits.<br/>• Step 2: Total lines = Cache Size / Block Size = 64 KB / 32 B = 65,536 / 32 = 2048 lines.<br/>• Step 3: Number of sets S = 2048 / 4 = 512 sets = 2^9 ⇒ Set Index s = 9 bits.<br/>• Step 4: Tag bits = Total bits - Set bits - Offset bits = 32 - 9 - 5 = 18 bits.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Offset = 5 bits, Set Index = 9 bits, Tag = 18 bits.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Tag Directory Size</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Tag directory memory size = (Total Lines) × (Tag bits + Valid bit). Remember to multiply by Total Lines, NOT the number of sets!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('COA', 3): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: Doing Laundry (Pipelining Principle)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Imagine washing 4 loads of laundry: Wash (30 min), Dry (40 min), Fold (20 min). If you do them one by one, 4 loads take 4 × 90 = 360 min (6 hours!). But in a pipeline: while load 1 is in the dryer, load 2 enters the washer! The dryer is the bottleneck (40 min). Once running, one clean basket finishes every 40 minutes, cutting total time in half!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: Passing Ingredients Direct from Frying Pan (Operand Forwarding)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Step 1 cooks onions; Step 2 needs cooked onions to make sauce. If Step 2 waits until Step 1 is plated and stored in the fridge, you waste time. Operand Forwarding (Bypassing) takes the hot onions directly from the frying pan and dumps them into Step 2\'s pot immediately!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: The Highway Fog Fork (Branch Prediction)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Driving fast toward a fork in the road in dense fog. If you stop the car to read the sign, traffic halts (Branch Stall). Instead, you guess \'keep going left\'. If your guess was right, you didn\'t lose any speed. If wrong, you stop, reverse, and take the right fork (Branch Penalty).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>12.1 Pipeline Clock Time & Speedup Formula</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          For a k-stage pipeline executing n instructions:<br/>• <b>Clock Cycle Time (T_pipe):</b> max(stage_delays) + register_delay.<br/>• <b>Total Clock Cycles:</b> k + (n - 1) cycles.<br/>• <b>Ideal Speedup:</b> S_max = k (number of stages, as n becomes very large).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Always add the pipeline buffer register delay to the maximum stage delay: T_clk = max(stages) + t_buffer!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>12.2 The 3 Pipeline Hazards & Solutions</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Structural Hazard:</b> Two instructions fight for same hardware (e.g. single memory port). Fix: Separate Instruction and Data Caches.<br/>• <b>Data Hazard (RAW):</b> Reading before writing. Fix: <b>Operand Forwarding</b>.<br/>• <b>Control Hazard:</b> Caused by branch instructions. Fix: Branch Prediction and delayed branching.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Operand forwarding CANNOT eliminate the stall between a LOAD instruction and an immediate ALU instruction using that value (Load-Use Hazard)! 1 stall cycle is still required.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Calculating Speedup\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> A 5-stage pipeline has stage delays of 150, 120, 180, 160, and 140 ps. Buffer delay = 20 ps. Non-pipelined total delay = 750 ps. Find speedup for 1000 instructions.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Non-pipelined time = 1000 × 750 ps = 750,000 ps.<br/>• Step 2: Pipelined clock cycle T = max(150, 120, 180, 160, 140) + 20 = 180 + 20 = 200 ps.<br/>• Step 3: Pipelined cycles = k + n - 1 = 5 + 1000 - 1 = 1004 cycles.<br/>• Step 4: Pipelined time = 1004 × 200 ps = 200,800 ps.<br/>• Step 5: Speedup S = 750,000 / 200,800 ≈ 3.735.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Speedup S = 3.74x.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Average CPI with Stalls</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          CPI_avg = 1 + (Stalls per instruction). Average execution time per instruction = CPI_avg × T_clk.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('PDS', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: Russian Nesting Dolls (Recursion & Call Stack)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Opening nesting dolls: You open doll #3 to find doll #2, then doll #1 (Base Case). At doll #1, you stop opening and start assembling them back! Every recursive function call places a box on the Call Stack; hitting the base case starts popping boxes off in reverse order.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: House Address on a Sticky Note (Pointers)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Instead of carrying your whole house to your friend (Pass by Value: copying data), you write your house address on a tiny sticky note (Pointer: `int *p = &x`). Your friend reads the address and visits your house to repaint a wall (Dereferencing: `*p = 50`). Changes happen directly to the original variable!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: The Persistent Locker (Static Variables)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Normally, local variables in a function are wiped clean when the function finishes. But a `static` variable is like a bolted metal locker: its value survives between function calls! The next time you call the function, it picks up right where it left off.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>13.1 Pointers & Array Equivalence in C</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • An array name `arr` is a pointer to its first element: `arr == &arr[0]`.<br/>• <b>Subscript Rule:</b> `arr[i] == *(arr + i) == *(i + arr) == i[arr]`. (In C, `3[arr]` is completely valid!).<br/>• `*p++` means: read `*p` first, then increment pointer `p`.<br/>• `(*p)++` means: increment the actual integer value that `p` points to.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: When an array is passed to a function, it decays to a simple pointer! Inside the function, `sizeof(arr)` gives the size of a pointer (4 or 8 bytes), NOT the full array size.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>13.2 Short-Circuit Evaluation in Logic</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • In `(A && B)`: If A is False (0), B is NEVER EXECUTED!<br/>• In `(A || B)`: If A is True (1), B is NEVER EXECUTED!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Watch out for increment operators inside logical conditions: `if (a == 0 && ++b > 0)`: since `a==0` might be false, `++b` never happens!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Tracing Static Recursion\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> What is printed by:\nint f(int n) {\n  static int r = 0;\n  if (n <= 0) return 1;\n  if (n > 3) { r = n; return f(n - 2) + 2; }\n  return f(n - 1) + r;\n}\nint main() { printf("%d", f(5)); }\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Call f(5): n=5 (>3) ⇒ static r becomes 5. Calls f(3) + 2.<br/>• Step 2: Inside f(3): n=3 (not >3) ⇒ Calls f(2) + r.<br/>• Step 3: Inside f(2): Calls f(1) + r.<br/>• Step 4: Inside f(1): Calls f(0) + r.<br/>• Step 5: Inside f(0): Hits base case, returns 1.<br/>• Step 6: Unwind f(1): 1 + 5 = 6. Unwind f(2): 6 + 5 = 11. Unwind f(3): 11 + 5 = 16.<br/>• Step 7: Unwind f(5): f(3) + 2 = 16 + 2 = 18.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Output = 18.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Undefined Behavior</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Expressions like `i = i++ + ++i` modify a variable multiple times without a sequence point. In C standard, this is UNDEFINED BEHAVIOR.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('PDS', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: Browser Back Button vs Printer Queue (Stack vs Queue)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Stack (LIFO: Last-In First-Out): Like a stack of plates. The last web page you opened is the first one that pops back when you hit the \'Back\' button.\n• Queue (FIFO: First-In First-Out): Like people standing in a ticket line. The first person to arrive is the first person served. Network printers use queues so the first document submitted prints first.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: Circular Buffet Counter (Circular Queue)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          In a linear queue, when people leave the front, empty seats open up, but you can\'t seat new diners if the rear pointer hit the end of the line. A Circular Queue wraps the pointer back to index 0 using modulo arithmetic `(rear + 1) % Capacity`, keeping 100% of seats usable.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: The Scavenger Hunt Clue Slips (Linked Lists)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          An array is like 10 adjacent hotel lockers: fast lookup by number, but impossible to expand if neighbor lockers are occupied. A Linked List is like a treasure hunt: each clue card holds an item and the GPS coordinate of where the next clue is hidden. Inserting someone in the middle takes O(1) time without moving anyone else!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>14.1 Infix, Postfix & Stack Applications</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Infix:</b> `A + B` &nbsp;|&nbsp; <b>Postfix:</b> `A B +` (No parentheses needed!).<br/>• <b>Evaluating Postfix with Stack:</b> Read left to right. Push numbers. When an operator arrives, pop two numbers (op2 = pop(), op1 = pop()), calculate `op1 op op2`, and push result back.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: In postfix evaluation, the FIRST popped element is operand 2 (the right-hand side operand)! Order matters for subtraction and division: op1 - op2.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>14.2 Queue Boundary Conditions</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • In a Circular Queue of size N (leaving 1 slot empty):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Full condition:</b> `(rear + 1) % N == front`.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Empty condition:</b> `front == rear`.<br/>• Max items stored = N - 1.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Minimum stacks needed to implement a Queue = 2. Minimum queues needed to implement a Stack = 2.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Evaluating Postfix Expression\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> Evaluate the postfix expression: `8 2 3 * + 4 -` using a stack.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Push 8, Push 2, Push 3. Stack = [8, 2, 3].<br/>• Step 2: Encounter \'*\': op2 = 3, op1 = 2 ⇒ 2 × 3 = 6. Push 6. Stack = [8, 6].<br/>• Step 3: Encounter \'+\': op2 = 6, op1 = 8 ⇒ 8 + 6 = 14. Push 14. Stack = [14].<br/>• Step 4: Push 4. Stack = [14, 4].<br/>• Step 5: Encounter \'-\': op2 = 4, op1 = 14 ⇒ 14 - 4 = 10. Push 10. Stack = [10].\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Final Result = 10.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Reversing a Linked List</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          To reverse a singly linked list in-place, always save `curr->next` in a temporary pointer BEFORE setting `curr->next = prev`, otherwise you will lose the rest of the list!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('PDS', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: High-Low Number Guessing Game (Binary Search Tree)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          If a friend picks a number between 1 and 100, you don\'t guess 1, 2, 3, 4 (Linear search: slow!). You guess 50. If they say \'Higher\', you immediately eliminate numbers 1 to 50 in ONE step! A Binary Search Tree does this on every step: left child is smaller, right child is bigger, finding any item among 1,000,000 items in just 20 checks (log2(1,000,000) ≈ 20).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: Hospital Emergency Room Triage (Priority Queue & Heap)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          In a hospital ER, patients are not treated by who arrived first. A patient with a severe heart attack is rushed ahead of someone with a mild headache. A Max-Heap keeps the highest-priority patient sitting at the very top (the root). When they enter surgery, the heap re-adjusts in just O(log n) time.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Balancing a Hanging Baby Mobile (AVL Tree Rotations)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          If you hang too many toys on the right branch of a mobile, it tips over and crashes. An AVL tree measures the height difference between left and right branches (Balance Factor). If it tilts by more than 1 level, it does a quick rotational swing (LL, RR, LR, RL) to restore perfect balance in O(1) time.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>15.1 Binary Tree Properties & Traversal Invariants</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • In any full/strict binary tree: <b>Leaf nodes L = Internal nodes I + 1</b>.<br/>• <b>BST Inorder Invariant:</b> Inorder traversal (Left, Root, Right) of a BST is ALWAYS in <b>STRICTLY ASCENDING SORTED ORDER</b>!<br/>• <b>Reconstructing Trees:</b> You CANNOT uniquely build a tree without INORDER traversal! (Need Inorder + Preorder OR Inorder + Postorder).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Given only Preorder and Postorder, you can build a unique tree ONLY if every non-leaf node has strictly 2 children.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>15.2 Binary Heap Properties</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          A Binary Heap is a <b>Complete Binary Tree</b> stored in an array.<br/>• Parent of node i is at ⌊i/2⌋. Left child is at 2i, Right child is at 2i + 1.<br/>• <b>Build-Heap Time:</b> Takes <b>O(n) time</b> (NOT O(n log n)!).<br/>• Insert / Delete-Root takes O(log n) time.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: In a Min-Heap with n elements, where is the MAXIMUM element? It is always in one of the LEAF nodes (between indices ⌊n/2⌋ + 1 and n)!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Finding Postorder from BST Preorder\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> Given Preorder traversal of a BST: [40, 20, 10, 30, 60, 50, 70]. What is its Postorder traversal?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: For a BST, Inorder is simply the sorted keys: Inorder = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70].<br/>• Step 2: Preorder first element is Root = 40.<br/>• Step 3: Keys before 40 in Inorder form Left Subtree: [10, 20, 30]. Keys after 40 form Right Subtree: [50, 60, 70].<br/>• Step 4: Left subtree root is 20 (left child 10, right child 30). Postorder = [10, 30, 20].<br/>• Step 5: Right subtree root is 60 (left child 50, right child 70). Postorder = [50, 70, 60].<br/>• Step 6: Combine: [10, 30, 20, 50, 70, 60, 40].\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Postorder Traversal = [10, 30, 20, 50, 70, 60, 40].\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Number of Structurally Distinct Binary Trees</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Number of structurally distinct binary trees with n unlabeled nodes is the Catalan Number: C_n = (1 / (n + 1)) × C(2n, n). For n=3, C_3 = 5 trees.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('ALGO', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: Carrier Pigeon vs Slow Internet (Big-O Growth)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          If you need to send 10 Terabytes of video across the city: sending it over slow 10 MB/s internet takes 12 days (O(n): time scales with data size!). But if you strap an SSD to a carrier pigeon, the pigeon flies across the city in 30 minutes whether the drive has 1 Megabyte or 10 Terabytes (O(1): constant time!). As data grows toward infinity, O(1) always beats O(n).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: The CEO Task Delegation (Master Theorem)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          A CEO splits a big project into \'a\' smaller sub-tasks of size \'n/b\'. Upper management does f(n) work to organize them, while the worker army at the leaves does n^(log_b a) work. The Master Theorem simply compares: Who does more work? If the worker army dominates, time is Θ(n^(log_b a)). If both do equal work, add a log n factor!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: The King\'s Chessboard Wheat Grains (Exponential Explosion)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          The inventor of chess asked the King for 1 grain of wheat on square 1, 2 grains on square 2, 4 on square 3, doubling every square: 2^n. By square 64, the wheat required was more than the entire planet\'s harvest for 2,000 years! Exponential O(2^n) algorithms become impossibly slow for even moderate inputs.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>16.1 Asymptotic Notations: O, Ω, Θ</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Big-O (O):</b> Upper Bound (Worst-case ceiling: \'runs at most this slow\').<br/>• <b>Big-Omega (Ω):</b> Lower Bound (Best-case floor: \'runs at least this fast\').<br/>• <b>Big-Theta (Θ):</b> Tight Bound (Both upper and lower bound match!).<br/>• <b>Growth speed ranking:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1 < log n < √n < n < n log n < n² < n³ < 2^n < n! < n^n.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: log(n!) = Θ(n log n) by Stirling\'s formula. Never write Θ(n^n) for log(n!)!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>16.2 Master Theorem in 3 Easy Steps</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          For recurrence: <b>T(n) = a T(n/b) + f(n)</b>:<br/>1. Calculate critical value: <b>p = log_b(a)</b>.<br/>2. Compare n^p with f(n):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If n^p > f(n) ⇒ <b>T(n) = Θ(n^p)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If n^p == f(n) ⇒ <b>T(n) = Θ(n^p · log n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If n^p < f(n) ⇒ <b>T(n) = Θ(f(n))</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Master theorem cannot be used if b is not a constant (e.g. T(n) = 2T(√n) + 1) or if a is not a constant!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Solving Recurrence with Master Theorem\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> Solve: T(n) = 4 T(n/2) + n².\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Identify: a = 4, b = 2, f(n) = n².<br/>• Step 2: Compute critical exponent p = log_b(a) = log_2(4) = 2.<br/>• Step 3: So n^p = n^2.<br/>• Step 4: Compare: n^p is exactly equal to f(n) = n² (Case 2!).<br/>• Step 5: Multiply by log n: T(n) = Θ(n² log n).\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 T(n) = Θ(n² log n).\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Non-Polynomial Gap in Master Theorem</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          For T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n / log n, n^p = n. Here f(n) = n / log n is smaller than n^p, but NOT polynomially smaller (the difference is a log factor, not n^ε). Master theorem fails here!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('ALGO', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: Sorting Exam Papers by PIN Code (Radix Sort)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          A teacher sorting 10,000 student answer sheets doesn\'t compare paper A with paper B one by one (QuickSort). They put papers into 10 plastic trays based on the last digit (0 to 9), collect them, and repeat for the tens and hundreds digits. In just a few passes, all 10,000 papers are sorted in linear O(n) time!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: The Unlucky Card Shuffle (QuickSort Worst Case)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          QuickSort picks a \'pivot\' and puts smaller cards on left, bigger cards on right. If the cards are ALREADY sorted and you foolishly pick the first card as pivot, the left partition has 0 cards and the right has n-1 cards. Instead of halving, the depth becomes n, degrading to slow O(n²) time. Picking a random pivot solves this.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Finding Your Car in a Crowded Parking Lot (Hash Collisions)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          A Hash function assigns your car a parking spot based on license plate mod 100. If someone else already parked in your spot (Collision), Linear Probing tells you to park in the next open spot. But if lots of cars crowd into neighboring spots, you get Primary Clustering jams.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>17.1 Comparison Sorting Lower Bound & Stability</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Theoretical Limit:</b> Any comparison-based sort must take at least <b>Ω(n log n)</b> comparisons in worst case.<br/>• <b>Stable Sorts (Preserves order of equal items):</b> Merge Sort, Insertion Sort, Bubble Sort.<br/>• <b>Unstable Sorts:</b> QuickSort, HeapSort, Selection Sort.<br/>• <b>In-Place Sorts (O(1) extra space):</b> HeapSort, QuickSort, Insertion Sort. (MergeSort requires O(n) extra memory!).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: HeapSort is guaranteed O(n log n) in ALL cases (best, average, worst) and uses O(1) space, but it is NOT stable!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>17.2 Hashing & Collision Techniques</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Load Factor α:</b> α = n / m (n items / m slots).<br/>• <b>Separate Chaining:</b> Uses linked lists at each slot. Can have α > 1. Search time: Θ(1 + α).<br/>• <b>Open Addressing:</b> Must have α ≤ 1.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Linear Probing: `(h(k) + i) mod m` (Causes Primary Clustering).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Quadratic Probing: `(h(k) + c1·i + c2·i²) mod m` (Causes Secondary Clustering).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Double Hashing: `(h1(k) + i · h2(k)) mod m` (Best open addressing!).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: In Double Hashing, h2(k) must be relatively prime to hash table size m so it can probe every single slot.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Linear Probing\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> Insert keys [23, 34, 45, 12] into hash table of size 11 using h(k) = k mod 11 and Linear Probing. Which slot does 12 go into?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: 23 mod 11 = 1 ⇒ Slot 1 (free).<br/>• Step 2: 34 mod 11 = 1 ⇒ Collision at 1! Try slot 2 ⇒ Slot 2 (free).<br/>• Step 3: 45 mod 11 = 1 ⇒ Collision at 1! Slot 2 is also full! Try slot 3 ⇒ Slot 3 (free).<br/>• Step 4: 12 mod 11 = 1 ⇒ Collision at 1! Slots 2 and 3 are full! Try slot 4 ⇒ Slot 4 (free).\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Key 12 is placed into Slot 4.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: QuickSort Average vs Worst Case</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Average time of QuickSort is Θ(n log n). Worst case time is Θ(n²). Read the question carefully to see whether it asks for average or worst-case!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('ALGO', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: The ATM Cash Machine (Greedy Choice)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          When you withdraw ₹3,800 from an ATM, it dispenses the biggest notes first: ₹2000 (1 note), ₹500 (3 notes), ₹200 (1 note), ₹100 (1 note). Making the locally best choice at each step is the Greedy method. For standard banknotes, this guarantees the fewest total notes. But if notes were ₹1, ₹7, ₹10 and you needed ₹14: Greedy picks ₹10 + four ₹1s (5 notes), while Dynamic Programming finds ₹7 + ₹7 (just 2 notes!).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: The Math Homework Scratchpad (Dynamic Programming)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Calculating Fibonacci(50) by hand branches into an impossible tree of billions of steps. But a smart student keeps a scratchpad: once Fib(3) is calculated, write down \'2\'. Anytime you need Fib(3) again, just look up your scratchpad in 1 second! Dynamic Programming turns an impossible exponential O(2^n) explosion into a fast linear O(n) table lookup.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Packing Your Flight Carry-On Bag (0/1 Knapsack)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Airline carry-on limit is 15 kg. You have heavy items: laptop, DSLR camera, shoes. You cannot pack half a laptop—you either take the whole laptop or leave it (0 or 1 choice). Fractional items like gold dust can be solved greedily; solid 0/1 items require Dynamic Programming.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>18.1 Greedy Algorithms: Knapsack, Activity Selection & Huffman Coding</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Fractional Knapsack:</b> Sort by Value/Weight ratio. Greedy is optimal. Time: O(n log n).<br/>• <b>Activity Selection:</b> Sort by <b>EARLIEST FINISH TIME</b>. Pick next compatible task.<br/>• <b>Huffman Coding:</b> Combines two least frequent characters using a Min-Heap. Generates variable-length optimal prefix codes. Time: O(n log n).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: A Huffman tree for n characters is ALWAYS a FULL binary tree with exactly 2n - 1 total nodes (n leaves and n - 1 internal nodes)!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>18.2 Dynamic Programming: 0/1 Knapsack & Matrix Chain</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>0/1 Knapsack:</b> Table DP[i][w] = max(DP[i-1][w], val[i] + DP[i-1][w - wt[i]]). Time: O(n × W) (Pseudo-polynomial).<br/>• <b>Matrix Chain Multiplication:</b> Computes optimal order to multiply matrices to minimize scalar multiplications. Time: O(n³).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: 0/1 Knapsack is NP-Complete! Its DP solution runs in pseudo-polynomial time because W is measured in binary bits.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Multiplying 3 Matrices\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> Find minimum scalar multiplications to multiply A1(10 × 30), A2(30 × 5), and A3(5 × 60).\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Two possible parenthesizations: (A1 × A2) × A3 OR A1 × (A2 × A3).<br/>• Step 2: Option 1: ((A1 × A2) × A3):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;A1 × A2 takes 10 × 30 × 5 = 1,500 operations (result size 10×5).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Result × A3 takes 10 × 5 × 60 = 3,000 operations.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Total Option 1 = 1,500 + 3,000 = 4,500 multiplications.<br/>• Step 3: Option 2: (A1 × (A2 × A3)):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;A2 × A3 takes 30 × 5 × 60 = 9,000 operations (result size 30×60).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;A1 × Result takes 10 × 30 × 60 = 18,000 operations.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Total Option 2 = 9,000 + 18,000 = 27,000 multiplications.<br/>• Step 4: Minimum = min(4500, 27000) = 4,500.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Minimum scalar multiplications = 4,500 with grouping ((A1 A2) A3).\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Longest Common Subsequence Length</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          If characters match: LCS = 1 + DP[i-1][j-1]. If they don\'t match: LCS = max(DP[i-1][j], DP[i][j-1]). Never add 1 when characters differ!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('ALGO', 3): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: Connecting Fiber Optic Cable Between Cities (MST)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          A telecom company wants to connect 10 cities with fiber optic cable so everyone can communicate. Laying cable is expensive. Kruskal\'s algorithm sorts all possible cable routes from cheapest to most expensive, laying down the cheapest cable first, as long as it doesn\'t form a useless closed loop. It guarantees the absolute cheapest total network cost.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: Google Maps Shortest Driving Route (Dijkstra\'s Algorithm)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          When you want the fastest route home, Google Maps starts at your current location (distance 0) and ripples outward like water drops, visiting the nearest reachable intersection next. Because road travel times are never negative, once Dijkstra locks in an intersection, it has permanently found the best route.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Currency Exchange Arbitrage (Bellman-Ford & Negative Cycles)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Converting 1 USD to Euros, then to Yen, then back to 1.05 USD creates free money! In math, taking the negative logarithm of exchange rates turns this into a Negative Weight Cycle. Dijkstra fails completely when edges are negative. Bellman-Ford runs for V - 1 rounds: if distances keep dropping on round V, a negative cycle is detected!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>19.1 BFS vs DFS Traversals</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>BFS (Breadth-First):</b> Uses a <b>Queue</b>. Explores neighbors level by level. Finds shortest path in <b>unweighted graphs</b>. Time: O(V + E).<br/>• <b>DFS (Depth-First):</b> Uses a <b>Stack</b> (or recursion). Explores deep down each path. Detects cycles in directed graph via <b>Back Edges</b>. Time: O(V + E).<br/>• <b>Topological Sort:</b> Linear ordering of vertices in a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Topological sort is possible IF AND ONLY IF the graph has NO cycles (it must be a DAG)!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>19.2 Minimum Spanning Tree (MST): Kruskal vs Prim</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Kruskal\'s Algorithm:</b> Sorts all edges by weight, adds edge if no cycle (uses Disjoint Set Union). Time: <b>O(E log V)</b>.<br/>• <b>Prim\'s Algorithm:</b> Starts from 1 vertex and grows the tree by picking lightest connected edge (uses Min-Heap). Time: <b>O(E log V)</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: If all edge weights in a connected graph are strictly UNIQUE, the Minimum Spanning Tree is 100% UNIQUE!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>19.3 Shortest Path Algorithm Matrix</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Dijkstra:</b> Single Source. Works ONLY with <b>NON-NEGATIVE edge weights</b>. Greedy. Time: O((V + E) log V).<br/>• <b>Bellman-Ford:</b> Single Source. Handles <b>NEGATIVE edge weights</b> and detects negative cycles. Time: <b>O(V × E)</b>.<br/>• <b>Floyd-Warshall:</b> All-Pairs Shortest Path. Dynamic Programming. Time: <b>O(V³)</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Dijkstra CANNOT work with negative edge weights, even if there are no negative cycles!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Dijkstra Algorithm Trace\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> Graph edges: A→B(4), A→C(2), C→B(1), B→D(5), C→D(8). Find shortest distance from source A to all vertices.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Start at A: dist[A]=0, others=∞. Visited={}.<br/>• Step 2: Pick A(0): update dist[B]=4, dist[C]=2. Visited={A}.<br/>• Step 3: Pick smallest unvisited: C(2). Outgoing: C→B(1) ⇒ dist[B] = min(4, 2+1) = 3 (updated!). C→D(8) ⇒ dist[D] = min(∞, 2+8) = 10. Visited={A, C}.<br/>• Step 4: Pick smallest unvisited: B(3). Outgoing: B→D(5) ⇒ dist[D] = min(10, 3+5) = 8 (updated!). Visited={A, C, B}.<br/>• Step 5: Pick D(8). All visited.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Shortest distances from A: A=0, B=3, C=2, D=8.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Adding a Constant to Fix Negative Weights</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          You CANNOT make negative edges positive for Dijkstra by adding a constant +10 to all edges! Paths with 5 edges get penalized more than paths with 2 edges, completely changing the shortest path!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('TOC', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: The Soda Vending Machine (Finite Automaton)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          A soda can costs ₹20. The machine accepts ₹5 and ₹10 coins. The machine has no infinite hard drive; it only remembers 4 states: {₹0, ₹5, ₹10, ₹15}. When you reach state ₹20, the can drops (Final / Accepting State!). Because the machine only needs a small, fixed number of states, it is a Deterministic Finite Automaton (DFA).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: Email Format Checking on a Website (Regular Expressions)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          When you type your email into a website, a Regular Expression checks whether it has letters, an \'@\' symbol, and a domain like \'.com\'. The browser converts the regex into a tiny DFA and tests your letters one by one in milliseconds. If it ends in an accepting state, a green checkmark appears.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Merging Duplicate Rooms in a Hotel (DFA Minimization)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          If Hotel Room 201 and Room 202 have identical beds, identical Wi-Fi, and identical room service for every guest request, keeping them separate wastes space. The Table-Filling method finds states that behave identically for all future inputs and merges them into a single state, creating the smallest Minimal DFA.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>20.1 DFA vs NFA Equivalences</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>DFA:</b> Exactly ONE transition for every state and input symbol. Deterministic.<br/>• <b>NFA:</b> Can have 0, 1, or multiple transitions on the same symbol, or jump without input (ε-moves).<br/>• <b>Power:</b> <b>DFA and NFA have the EXACT SAME POWER</b>! Every NFA can be converted to an equivalent DFA with at most 2^n states.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: While DFA and NFA recognize the same languages, an NFA with n states might need up to 2^n states when converted to a minimal DFA (e.g. \'n-th symbol from right is 1\').\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>20.2 Regular Language Closure Properties</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          Regular languages are closed under <b>ALMOST EVERYTHING</b>:<br/>• Union (L1 ∪ L2) ✔<br/>• Intersection (L1 ∩ L2) ✔<br/>• Complement (~L) ✔<br/>• Concatenation (L1 · L2) ✔<br/>• Kleene Star (L*) ✔<br/>• Reversal (L^R) ✔\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: If L1 and L2 are regular, any combination using Union, Intersection, or Complement is GUARANTEED to be regular!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>20.3 Arden\'s Theorem for Regular Expressions</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          If P and Q are regular expressions, and P does not contain ε:<br/>• The equation <b>R = Q + R P</b> has the unique solution: <b>R = Q P*</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: In Arden\'s theorem, R = Q + R P becomes Q P*. (Notice P* comes on the RIGHT!).\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: States for \'Binary Number Divisible by 3\'\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> How many states are in the minimal DFA that accepts binary strings whose decimal value is divisible by 3?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Any number divided by 3 can have only 3 remainders: 0, 1, or 2.<br/>• Step 2: Each remainder corresponds to one state: State 0 (remainder 0), State 1 (remainder 1), State 2 (remainder 2).<br/>• Step 3: State 0 is the start state AND the only accepting state (remainder 0).<br/>• Step 4: None of these 3 remainder states behave identically, so no states can be merged.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Minimum states = 3.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Dead / Trap State in DFA</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In a DFA, every state MUST have an outgoing arrow for every alphabet symbol! If a string becomes permanently invalid (e.g. starts with \'0\'), it goes to a Dead/Trap state. Don\'t forget to count the Dead State!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('TOC', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: Matching Parentheses in VS Code (Pushdown Automaton)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          When you write code with nested brackets `((a + b) * (c - d))`, your editor highlights matching pairs. A simple Finite Automaton cannot do this because brackets can be nested 50 levels deep. A Pushdown Automaton (PDA) solves this using an internal Stack: push on \'(\', pop on \')\'. If the stack is empty at the end, the code is balanced! Adding stack memory creates Context-Free Languages (CFL).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: The Two-Hand Scale Limit (Why a^n b^n c^n is NOT Context-Free)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Imagine a physical balance scale with 2 pans. You can easily balance a^n against b^n by pushing \'a\'s and popping for \'b\'s. But what if you have THREE things: a^n b^n c^n? The moment you pop all the \'a\'s to match \'b\'s, your stack is completely empty: the memory is gone! You have no way to remember \'n\' when counting \'c\'s. This is why a^n b^n c^n is NOT context-free.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Ambiguous Newspaper Headlines (Grammar Ambiguity)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          A headline reads: \'Enraged Cow Injures Farmer with Axe\'. Did the cow hold an axe, or did the farmer hold an axe? Two completely different parse trees exist for the same sentence! In programming, an ambiguous grammar causes compiler bugs. Grammars must be designed to have only 1 unique parse tree for every valid statement.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>21.1 Chomsky Normal Form (CNF) Derivation Steps</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          A grammar is in CNF if all rules look like <b>A → BC</b> or <b>A → a</b>.<br/>• <b>Derivation Steps:</b> To generate any string of length n (n ≥ 1) using a CNF grammar, it ALWAYS takes exactly <b>2n - 1 steps</b>!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: If a string has length 10, deriving it in CNF takes exactly 2(10) - 1 = 19 production steps. This is a direct 1-mark GATE question!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>21.2 Deterministic PDA (DPDA) vs Non-Deterministic PDA (NPDA)</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>DPDA (Deterministic CFL):</b> Recognizes languages like `w c w^R` (where \'c\' marks the middle). Closed under <b>Complement</b>!<br/>• <b>NPDA (CFL):</b> Recognizes languages like `w w^R` (even-length palindrome without middle marker: needs guessing).<br/>• <b>Closure warning:</b> CFLs are <b>NOT closed under Intersection or Complement</b>!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: If L1 and L2 are Context-Free, L1 ∩ L2 might NOT be Context-Free!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Proving a Language is Not Regular\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> Is L = { a^n b^n | n ≥ 0 } regular or context-free?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Check memory needed: To ensure number of \'a\'s equals number of \'b\'s, the machine must count arbitrarily high.<br/>• Step 2: A Finite Automaton has fixed finite states, so it cannot count arbitrary numbers. Hence, L is NOT regular.<br/>• Step 3: A Pushdown Automaton can push \'a\' onto a stack and pop for each \'b\'. When input ends, if stack is empty, accept.<br/>• Step 4: Since a PDA can accept it deterministically, L is a Deterministic Context-Free Language (DCFL).\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 L is Non-Regular, but it is DCFL (Context-Free).\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: DCFL vs CFL Complement</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          DCFL IS closed under complement. But general CFL is NOT closed under complement!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('TOC', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: Your Laptop as a Universal Turing Machine\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Before Alan Turing, a calculator was built to do only one thing (like an adding machine). Turing proved that you don\'t need a thousand different machines: you build ONE computer that reads a program and data from memory and executes it. Your laptop or phone is a Universal Turing Machine!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: The Infinite Antivirus Loop (The Halting Problem)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Can someone write a perfect antivirus that inspects ANY program and guarantees whether it will ever freeze in an infinite loop? Turing proved this is mathematically IMPOSSIBLE. If you give that tool a program that says \'Halt if the tool says I loop, and Loop if the tool says I halt\', the tool is trapped in a logical contradiction. The Halting Problem is Undecidable.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Nested Russian Dolls (Chomsky Hierarchy)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Languages fit inside each other like Russian dolls:\nRegular ⊂ DCFL ⊂ CFL ⊂ Context-Sensitive ⊂ Recursive (Decidable) ⊂ Recursively Enumerable (Turing Recognizable).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>22.1 Recursive (REC) vs Recursively Enumerable (RE)</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Recursive (REC / Decidable):</b> Turing machine ALWAYS halts and gives YES or NO in finite time (No infinite loops).<br/>• <b>Recursively Enumerable (RE / Semi-decidable):</b> Halts on YES, but might loop forever on NO.<br/>• <b>Complement Rule:</b> Language L is Decidable IF AND ONLY IF <b>both L and ~L are RE</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: If L is RE but not Recursive, then its complement ~L is strictly NOT RE!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>22.2 Rice\'s Theorem in Plain Words</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          Any question about the <b>LANGUAGE recognized by a Turing Machine</b> (like \'Is L(M) empty?\', \'Is L(M) regular?\', \'Does it accept string 01?\') is <b>UNDECIDABLE</b>.<br/>• If it\'s a property about the code itself (e.g. \'Does TM have 5 states?\'), it is DECIDABLE.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: If a GATE question asks if a non-trivial property of the language of a Turing Machine is decidable, the answer is ALWAYS Undecidable by Rice\'s Theorem!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Decidability Checklist\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> Is the Emptiness problem (Is L = ∅?) decidable for: (1) Regular languages, (2) Context-Free languages, (3) Turing Machines?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: For Regular languages: Check if there is a path from start state to any final state in the DFA. Easily solvable ⇒ DECIDABLE.<br/>• Step 2: For Context-Free languages: Check if start symbol can derive any terminal string. Solvable in polynomial time ⇒ DECIDABLE.<br/>• Step 3: For Turing Machines: Checking if a TM accepts nothing is equivalent to the Halting problem ⇒ UNDECIDABLE.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Regular: Decidable | CFL: Decidable | TM: Undecidable.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Equivalence of CFLs</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Testing if two Regular languages are equal (L1 == L2) is DECIDABLE. But testing if two Context-Free languages are equal is UNDECIDABLE!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('CD', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: Reading Words in a Book (Lexical Analyzer / Tokenizer)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          When you read, you don\'t look at single ink dots or spaces. Your brain groups letters into words: \'int\' (Keyword), \'score\' (Variable Identifier), \'=\' (Assignment Operator), \'100\' (Number). The Lexical Analyzer does this: it strips out spaces and comments, and converts characters into clean Tokens for the parser.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: Train Track Signal (LL(1) 1-Token Lookahead)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          An LL(1) parser is like a train driver who only looks at 1 signal light ahead. If green, go track A; if yellow, go track B. It never needs to put the train in reverse (Zero backtracking!). But if two different tracks share the same start signal, the train crashes. Left Factoring fixes this by splitting the shared track.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: The Mirror Loop of Left Recursion\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          If a recipe rule says: \'To bake a cake, first bake a cake, then add sugar\' (A → Aα), you will be stuck in an infinite loop without ever cracking an egg! Top-down parsers get stuck in infinite loops when grammars have Left Recursion.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>23.1 Tokens, Patterns & Lexical Errors</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Lexeme:</b> The exact characters in code (e.g. `count`, `3.14`).<br/>• <b>Token:</b> The abstract category (e.g. `<ID>`, `<NUM>`).<br/>• <b>Longest Match (Maximal Munch):</b> Scans the longest valid token (e.g. `---` is scanned as `--` then `-`).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Undeclared variables or missing semicolons are NOT lexical errors! They are Syntax or Semantic errors.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>23.2 FIRST and FOLLOW Rules</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>FIRST(X):</b> All terminals that can appear as the first symbol of a string derived from X.<br/>• <b>FOLLOW(A):</b> All terminals that can appear immediately to the right of non-terminal A.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Put `$` in FOLLOW(Start_Symbol).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. If rule is `A → α B β`, add (FIRST(β) - {ε}) to FOLLOW(B).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. If rule is `A → α B` or β derives ε, add all of FOLLOW(A) to FOLLOW(B).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: FOLLOW sets NEVER contain ε! The empty string ε can be in FIRST, but NEVER in FOLLOW.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>23.3 LL(1) Grammar Conditions</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          A grammar is LL(1) if for every rule A → α | β:<br/>1. FIRST(α) and FIRST(β) do not share any common terminals.<br/>2. If α derives ε, then FIRST(β) and FOLLOW(A) do not share any common terminals.<br/>• <b>Never LL(1):</b> Ambiguous grammars, Left-Recursive grammars, and Non-Left-Factored grammars are NEVER LL(1)!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: If an LL(1) parsing table cell contains 2 productions, the grammar is NOT LL(1)!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Computing FIRST and FOLLOW\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> Grammar: S → A B, A → a | ε, B → b. Find FIRST and FOLLOW for S, A, B.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: FIRST(B) = {b}.<br/>• Step 2: FIRST(A) = {a, ε}.<br/>• Step 3: FIRST(S): S → AB. Starts with A, so takes FIRST(A) - {ε} = {a}. Since A can be ε, it also takes FIRST(B) = {b}. Thus FIRST(S) = {a, b}.<br/>• Step 4: FOLLOW(S) = {$} (since S is start symbol).<br/>• Step 5: FOLLOW(A): In S → AB, B follows A ⇒ FOLLOW(A) = FIRST(B) = {b}.<br/>• Step 6: FOLLOW(B): In S → AB, nothing follows B ⇒ FOLLOW(B) = FOLLOW(S) = {$}.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 FIRST(S)={a,b}, FIRST(A)={a,ε}, FIRST(B)={b} | FOLLOW(S)={$}, FOLLOW(A)={b}, FOLLOW(B)={$}.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Eliminating Left Recursion</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          For `A → Aα | β`, the non-recursive form is `A → βA\'`, `A\' → αA\' | ε`. Remember: β comes first, NOT α!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('CD', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: Assembling Lego Bricks (Shift-Reduce Parsing)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Bottom-up parsing starts with scattered Lego pieces on the table. You pick up a piece from the input (Shift). When 4 pieces on your table form a completed sub-assembly matching a blueprint rule, you snap them together into a block (Reduce). You keep shifting and reducing until one complete Lego house is built (Accept!).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: The Four Models of Night-Vision Goggles (LR Parsers)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • LR(0): Blind reduction without looking ahead.\n• SLR(1): Cheap flashlight: reduces only if lookahead is in FOLLOW set.\n• LALR(1): Smart infrared goggles: tracks lookahead while merging identical states to keep memory small (Used by Yacc and Bison!).\n• CLR(1): Full military satellite radar: most powerful, but huge table size.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Dangling Else Dilemma (Shift-Reduce Conflict)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          In `if (a) if (b) s1 else s2`: should `else` attach to the inner `if` (Shift) or close the outer `if` (Reduce)? Both choices are valid grammar moves! Most compilers choose Shift so `else` pairs with the closest preceding `if`.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>24.1 LR Parser Family Hierarchy</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Number of States:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>State Count(LR(0)) == State Count(SLR(1)) == State Count(LALR(1)) < State Count(CLR(1))</b>.<br/>• <b>Parsing Power:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>LL(1) < SLR(1) < LALR(1) < CLR(1)</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: LALR(1) and SLR(1) have the EXACT SAME number of states as LR(0) because LALR only merges states that share identical cores!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>24.2 Conflicts When Merging States to LALR(1)</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          When merging CLR(1) states to form LALR(1):<br/>• <b>Shift-Reduce Conflicts:</b> CAN NEVER BE CREATED by merging!<br/>• <b>Reduce-Reduce Conflicts:</b> <b>CAN BE CREATED</b> by merging!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: If a grammar is CLR(1), it might NOT be LALR(1) because state merging can create a Reduce-Reduce conflict.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Comparing Parser States\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> A grammar has 10 states in LR(0). How many states will it have in SLR(1) and LALR(1)?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: SLR(1) uses the exact same states as LR(0), only adding FOLLOW checks to reduction cells.<br/>• Step 2: LALR(1) merges CLR(1) states down to the exact LR(0) core.<br/>• Step 3: Therefore, State Count is identical for all three: 10 states.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 SLR(1) has 10 states, LALR(1) has 10 states.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Shift-Reduce Conflict Resolution</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Never declare a grammar ambiguous just because it has an S-R conflict. Declaring operator precedence and associativity easily resolves the conflict.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('CD', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: Supermarket Billing Receipt (Synthesized Attributes)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          When calculating a receipt: Item 1 is ₹20, Item 2 is ₹50. The total sum ₹70 is computed by adding the values of its children line items. An attribute calculated from child values is called a Synthesized Attribute (S-Attributed). It flows upward naturally during parsing.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: One Grocery Trip for Milk and Bread (Common Subexpression)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          If your morning routine says: \'Drive to store and buy milk; then drive to store and buy bread\', you do 2 trips. A smart person combines them into 1 trip: buy both together! Common Subexpression Elimination detects identical calculations (`a = b + c; d = b + c;`) and reuses the first answer.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Moving Work Outside a Loop (Loop Invariant Code Motion)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          If a workout routine says: \'Do 100 pushups, and during every single pushup calculate 1000 × 50\', doing the math 100 times inside the loop is silly. Calculate 50,000 ONCE before you start, and just do the pushups!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>25.1 S-Attributed vs L-Attributed Definitions</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>S-Attributed:</b> Uses <b>ONLY synthesized attributes</b> (evaluated bottom-up on shift-reduce stack).<br/>• <b>L-Attributed:</b> Can use synthesized OR inherited attributes, but inherited attributes can depend ONLY on parents and <b>LEFT siblings</b> (never right siblings!).<br/>• Every S-attributed SDT is also L-attributed: <b>S-Attributed ⊂ L-Attributed</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: If an inherited attribute depends on a RIGHT sibling, it is NOT L-attributed!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>25.2 Liveness Analysis (GATE 2027 Syllabus Focus)</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          A variable is \'Live\' at point p if its current value will be read by some future instruction.<br/>• <b>Equation:</b> IN[B] = USE[B] ∪ (OUT[B] - DEF[B]).<br/>• This is a <b>BACKWARD data flow problem</b> (computed from program end to start).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: If a variable is redefined in a block before being read, its old value is \'killed\' and does NOT appear in USE[B].\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Basic Block Live Variables\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> Block B has instructions: (1) a = b + c, (2) d = a - 5. If OUT[B] = {d}, find IN[B].\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Check uses and defs from bottom to top:<br/>• &nbsp;&nbsp;Line 2 defines \'d\', uses \'a\'.<br/>• &nbsp;&nbsp;Line 1 defines \'a\', uses \'b\' and \'c\'.<br/>• Step 2: Variables needed from outside: \'b\' and \'c\' are used before definition. \'a\' is defined inside so it\'s not needed from outside.<br/>• Step 3: IN[B] = {b, c}.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 IN[B] = {b, c}.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Dead Code Elimination</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          If a variable `x = y + z` is calculated, but `x` is never used anywhere in the rest of the program, `x` is Dead Code and can be removed completely.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('OS', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: Separate Apartments vs Shared Kitchen (Process vs Thread)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Processes: Like two neighboring families in separate locked apartments. Family A cannot touch Family B\'s fridge (isolated memory, safe from crashes, but sharing needs IPC).\n• Threads: Like roommates sharing the same kitchen and living room (shared memory, super fast communication, but if one roommate sets the stove on fire, everyone in the apartment goes down!).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: Single Restroom Key (Mutex / Binary Semaphore)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          A coffee shop has one restroom and one physical brass key hanging at the counter. Customer 1 takes the key and locks the door (wait: key count drops from 1 to 0). Customer 2 arrives, sees no key, and waits. When Customer 1 returns the key (signal: key count goes back to 1), Customer 2 enters. This guarantees Mutual Exclusion.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: The Fork() Family Tree\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          When a process calls `fork()`, it creates an exact identical twin clone of itself! In the parent process, fork() returns the baby\'s ID (> 0). Inside the new child process, fork() returns 0. If you call fork() 3 times in a row, you end up with 2^3 = 8 processes.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>26.1 Critical Section: The 3 Mandatory Requirements</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          1. <b>Mutual Exclusion:</b> Only 1 process in critical section at a time.<br/>2. <b>Progress:</b> If critical section is free, only processes wanting to enter decide who goes next. No infinite waiting.<br/>3. <b>Bounded Waiting:</b> There is a limit on how many times others can enter before you get your turn (Prevents Starvation).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Strict Alternation (using a turn variable) guarantees Mutual Exclusion, but FAILS PROGRESS because if process 0 stops, process 1 can never enter again!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>26.2 Counting Semaphore Interpretation</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>wait(S):</b> `S = S - 1; if (S < 0) block();`<br/>• <b>signal(S):</b> `S = S + 1; if (S <= 0) wakeup();`<br/>• <b>Meaning:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If S ≥ 0: S is the count of <b>free available resources</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If S < 0: |S| is the count of <b>processes waiting in the blocked queue</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Formula for final value: S_final = S_initial - (number of wait calls) + (number of signal calls).\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Counting Semaphore Value\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> A semaphore is initialized to 8. A sequence of 15 wait() and 10 signal() operations occur. What is the final value of the semaphore, and how many processes are blocked?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Write formula: S_final = S_init - wait_count + signal_count.<br/>• Step 2: Plug in: S_final = 8 - 15 + 10 = 8 - 5 = 3.<br/>• Step 3: Since S_final = +3 (positive), exactly 3 resources are free.<br/>• Step 4: Blocked queue has 0 processes.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Final value = 3, Blocked processes = 0.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Counting Child Processes</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          If a question asks: \'How many CHILD processes are created by n fork() calls?\', the answer is 2^n - 1 (subtract 1 for the original parent!).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('OS', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: Supermarket Express Checkout (SJF Scheduling)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Waiting in a grocery line behind someone with 3 overflowing carts takes 45 minutes (Convoy Effect in FCFS). You only want to buy 1 bottle of water (10 seconds!). Shortest Job First (SJF) lets the water bottle customer go first, drastically slashing average waiting time for everyone.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: The Bank Loan Safety Check (Banker\'s Algorithm)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          A bank has ₹10,000 cash. Three customers have credit limits. Before handing out a ₹4,000 loan, the manager checks: \'If I give this cash, does there still exist at least one order where every customer can finish and return the money?\' If yes, the state is SAFE. If not, the loan is paused to prevent Deadlock.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Skyscraper Elevator (SCAN Disk Scheduling)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          An elevator in a tall building doesn\'t jump randomly between floor 3, floor 80, and floor 5. It sweeps smoothly up to the top floor servicing every request along the way, then sweeps back down. SCAN (Elevator algorithm) prevents mechanical disk head thrashing.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>27.1 CPU Scheduling Formulas & SRTF</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Turnaround Time (TAT):</b> Completion Time - Arrival Time.<br/>• <b>Waiting Time (WT):</b> Turnaround Time - Burst Time.<br/>• <b>SRTF (Shortest Remaining Time First):</b> Preemptive SJF. <b>Provably gives minimum average waiting time</b> of all scheduling algorithms!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: In Round Robin scheduling, if a new process arrives at the exact same instant an old process finishes its time quantum, put the NEW process in the queue FIRST!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>27.2 The 4 Coffman Deadlock Conditions</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          Deadlock can occur only if ALL 4 conditions hold together:<br/>1. <b>Mutual Exclusion:</b> Resources cannot be shared.<br/>2. <b>Hold and Wait:</b> Holding resources while asking for more.<br/>3. <b>No Preemption:</b> Resources cannot be forcibly taken.<br/>4. <b>Circular Wait:</b> Circular chain of waiting processes.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Break even ONE of the 4 conditions, and Deadlock is 100% PREVENTED!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Banker\'s Algorithm Need Matrix\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> Process P1 has Max Claim = (7, 5, 3) and Current Allocation = (2, 1, 1). What is its remaining Need?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Need = Max - Allocation.<br/>• Step 2: Resource A: 7 - 2 = 5.<br/>• Step 3: Resource B: 5 - 1 = 4.<br/>• Step 4: Resource C: 3 - 1 = 2.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Need(P1) = (5, 4, 2).\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Resource Graph Cycles</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          A cycle in a Resource Allocation Graph guarantees deadlock ONLY IF every resource has strictly 1 single instance! If resources have multiple instances, a cycle might NOT cause deadlock.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('OS', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: Library Study Desk vs Dark Basement (Paging & Page Fault)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          A college library has 100,000 books in the basement, but your study desk only fits 4 books (RAM Frames). If the chapter you need is on the desk -> Page Hit (1 second). If the book is down in the cold basement -> Page Fault: reading pauses, the librarian rides the slow elevator, retrieves the book, and puts it on your desk (takes 10 milliseconds = 10,000,000 nanoseconds!).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: The Bigger Parking Lot Paradox (Belady\'s Anomaly)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Normally, adding more capacity helps. But under FIFO page replacement, giving a program 4 memory frames instead of 3 can actually CAUSE MORE PAGE FAULTS for certain page sequences! FIFO kicks out pages blindly without knowing if you will need them in the next second.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: The Tree of Disk Pointers (UNIX Inode)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          A UNIX file has 12 direct pointers (pointing straight to data blocks for small files: instant access). For huge movies, it uses 1 Singly Indirect and 1 Doubly Indirect pointer (a table pointing to tables of blocks), scaling up to Gigabytes effortlessly.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>28.1 Paging & Effective Memory Access Time (EMAT)</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          Virtual address is split into: [ Page Number (p) | Offset (d) ].<br/>• <b>With TLB:</b> EMAT = h × (t_tlb + t_mem) + (1 - h) × (t_tlb + 2 × t_mem).<br/>• <b>Page Table Size:</b> (Total Pages) × (Page Table Entry Size).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Watch units in EMAT! Memory access is in nanoseconds (ns), but Page Fault penalty is in MILLISECONDS (ms = 10^6 ns). Convert to the same unit before calculating!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>28.2 Page Replacement Algorithms</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>FIFO:</b> Replaces oldest page. Suffers from Belady\'s anomaly.<br/>• <b>Optimal:</b> Replaces page not needed for longest time in FUTURE. Minimum page faults, but impossible in practice.<br/>• <b>LRU:</b> Replaces page not used for longest time in PAST. Stack algorithm: <b>IMMUNE to Belady\'s Anomaly</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: LRU and Optimal algorithms can NEVER suffer from Belady\'s Anomaly!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: EMAT Calculation\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> TLB search time = 20 ns, Main memory access time = 100 ns. TLB hit ratio = 90%. What is the Effective Memory Access Time?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: On Hit (90%): TLB search + 1 memory access = 20 + 100 = 120 ns.<br/>• Step 2: On Miss (10%): TLB search + Page Table memory access + Data memory access = 20 + 100 + 100 = 220 ns.<br/>• Step 3: EMAT = (0.90 × 120 ns) + (0.10 × 220 ns) = 108 + 22 = 130 ns.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 EMAT = 130 ns.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Internal vs External Fragmentation</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Paging eliminates External Fragmentation completely! But it can still suffer from Internal Fragmentation in the very last page of a process.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('DB', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: Hospital Blueprints vs Real Rooms (ER Model to Tables)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Before building a hospital, you draw a sketch of Doctors, Patients, and Rooms (ER Diagram). When building the hospital, you pour real concrete rooms with ID numbers (Relational Tables). Because one Doctor sees many Patients, you don\'t build a new hospital: you put the Doctor\'s ID on the Patient\'s medical chart (Foreign Key on the N-side!).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: Slicing Vegetables (Selection σ vs Projection π)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Imagine a basket of potatoes and carrots. Selection (σ) acts like a filter: it picks only the red carrots and throws away potatoes (filters ROWS horizontally). Projection (π) acts like a peeler: it keeps only the weight tag and throws away the color and farm origin (slices COLUMNS vertically).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: The Mystery Answer on an Exam Sheet (NULL in SQL)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          In SQL, `NULL` does NOT mean zero, and it does NOT mean empty text. It means \'UNKNOWN / MISSING\'. If a student\'s marks are NULL, what is `marks > 50`? It is UNKNOWN! In SQL three-valued logic, `NULL = NULL` is NOT True—it is UNKNOWN! That is why writing `WHERE marks = NULL` returns zero rows; you must write `WHERE marks IS NULL`.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>29.1 ER to Relational Table Conversion Rules</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>1:1 Relationship:</b> Combine into 2 tables, or 1 table if total participation.<br/>• <b>1:N Relationship:</b> Relationship table can be merged into the <b>\'N\' side table</b> (Foreign key goes to N-side!).<br/>• <b>M:N Relationship:</b> CANNOT be merged! Requires a <b>separate table</b> with primary keys of both entities.<br/>• <b>Weak Entity:</b> Always merged with its identifying relationship into 1 table.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: In a 1:N relationship, the Foreign Key ALWAYS goes to the \'N\' (Many) side, NEVER the \'1\' side!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>29.2 Relational Algebra Core Operators</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Selection (σ):</b> Filters rows based on condition: σ_age>20(Student).<br/>• <b>Projection (π):</b> Selects columns: π_name,roll(Student). Automatically eliminates duplicate rows!<br/>• <b>Cartesian Product (R × S):</b> Combines every row of R with every row of S.<br/>• <b>Natural Join (R ⋈ S):</b> Joins matching common columns.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Relational algebra automatically eliminates duplicate rows (Set semantics). SQL keeps duplicates (Bag semantics) unless you write DISTINCT!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>29.3 SQL Rules & NULL Behaviors</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • `WHERE` filters rows BEFORE grouping.<br/>• `HAVING` filters groups AFTER `GROUP BY`.<br/>• Aggregate functions (SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX) <b>IGNORE NULLs completely</b>.<br/>• Exception: `COUNT(*)` counts ALL rows, including rows containing NULLs!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: If a subquery inside `NOT IN (SELECT id FROM ...)` returns even ONE NULL, the entire outer query returns ZERO rows!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Minimum Tables for ER Diagram\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> Entities E1 and E2 have a 1:N relationship R1. E2 and E3 have an M:N relationship R2. What is the minimum number of relational tables needed?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: E1 gets 1 table: Table(E1).<br/>• Step 2: 1:N relationship R1 merges into N-side table E2: Table(E2) with E1\'s key as foreign key.<br/>• Step 3: Entity E3 gets 1 table: Table(E3).<br/>• Step 4: M:N relationship R2 MUST have its own independent table: Table(R2).<br/>• Step 5: Total tables = Table(E1) + Table(E2) + Table(E3) + Table(R2) = 4 tables.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Minimum tables = 4.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: COUNT(*) vs COUNT(column)</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          COUNT(*) counts every row in the table. COUNT(column_name) counts only rows where that specific column is NOT NULL!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('DB', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: Repeated College Roster Rows (Database Anomalies)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Imagine a college spreadsheet repeating Student Name, HOD Name, and HOD Mobile Number 5,000 times. If the HOD changes their phone number, updating 5,000 rows by hand is crazy (Update Anomaly). If you miss 2 rows, data becomes corrupted. If the last student drops out of a course and you delete the row, you accidentally delete the course from the college (Deletion Anomaly)! Normalization splits data into clean, separate tables.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: The Driving License Master Key (Candidate Key)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Your driving license number determines your Name, Blood Group, and Address. Does your Blood Group determine your Name? No! An Attribute Closure (X+) starts with a column and sees how many other columns it unlocks. If X+ unlocks the whole table, X is a Candidate Key.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Disassembling a Lego Car (Lossless Join)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          When you split a big table into two smaller tables, you must be able to snap them back together using Natural Join without creating fake ghost rows that never existed (Lossless Join). The common column must be a Superkey in at least one of the tables.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>30.1 The Normal Forms in 4 Simple Rules</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>1NF:</b> Every cell holds a single atomic value (no lists or arrays in cells).<br/>• <b>2NF:</b> In 1NF and <b>NO PARTIAL DEPENDENCY</b> (no non-key column depends on just half of a composite key).<br/>• <b>3NF:</b> In 2NF and <b>NO TRANSITIVE DEPENDENCY</b>. For every FD `X → A`: either <b>X is a Superkey</b> OR <b>A is a Prime Attribute</b>.<br/>• <b>BCNF:</b> For every FD `X → A`, <b>X MUST BE A SUPERKEY</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Any relation with only 2 attributes is ALWAYS in BCNF! (Direct GATE shortcut).\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>30.2 Lossless Join Decomposition Test</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          Splitting relation R into R1 and R2 is Lossless if and only if:<br/>• <b>(R1 ∩ R2) → R1</b> &nbsp;OR&nbsp; <b>(R1 ∩ R2) → R2</b>.<br/>In plain words: The common attribute must be a Superkey of at least one of the sub-tables!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: 3NF decomposition can ALWAYS achieve BOTH Lossless Join and Dependency Preservation. BCNF guarantees Lossless Join, but may NOT preserve dependencies!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Finding Highest Normal Form\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> Relation R(A, B, C) with FDs: { A → B, B → C }. What is the highest normal form of R?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Find candidate keys: (A)+ = {A, B, C} ⇒ Candidate Key is A. Prime attribute = {A}.<br/>• Step 2: Check 1NF: Atomic values ✔.<br/>• Step 3: Check 2NF: No partial dependency (key A has only 1 attribute) ✔.<br/>• Step 4: Check 3NF: In B → C: is B a superkey? No. Is C a prime attribute? No. Both tests fail! Violates 3NF.<br/>• Step 5: Therefore, highest normal form is 2NF.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Highest Normal Form = 2NF.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Prime Attributes Can Save 3NF</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In 3NF, the rule is \'X is superkey OR A is prime\'. Even if X is NOT a superkey, as long as RHS attribute A is part of ANY candidate key, 3NF is NOT violated!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('DB', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: The Library Catalog vs Shelf Books (B+ Trees)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          An internal node of a B+ tree is like a catalog sign: \'Books A-F in aisle 1, G-M in aisle 2\'. The sign contains NO real books (no data records)—only index pointers! The real books live exclusively on the shelves (Leaf Nodes). All shelves are connected side-by-side with a rope (Doubly Linked List), letting you scan books sequentially without climbing back up to the catalog sign.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: ATM Money Transfer (ACID Properties)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          You transfer ₹5,000 from Savings to Checking. Atomicity (All or Nothing): if power cuts mid-transfer, money isn\'t lost—it resets back. Consistency: total money remains invariant. Isolation: your friend checking balance doesn\'t see partial amounts. Durability: once \'Success\' displays, a crash cannot wipe the transaction.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Presidential Motorcade (Strict 2-Phase Locking - 2PL)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Growing Phase: acquire locks on every road ahead, NEVER release any lock. Shrinking Phase: once the car safely arrives and commits, unlock all roads at once. Strict 2PL guarantees Conflict Serializability and prevents Cascading Aborts.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>31.1 B+ Tree Node Capacity Formulas</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Internal Node of order p:</b> Holds p block pointers and p - 1 search keys.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>p × (Block Pointer Size) + (p - 1) × (Key Size) ≤ Block Size</b>.<br/>• Data pointers reside <b>EXCLUSIVELY in Leaf Nodes</b>!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Internal nodes in a B+ Tree contain NO record/data pointers, which allows much higher fan-out and shallower tree height compared to B-Trees.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>31.2 Conflict Serializability via Precedence Graph</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          Two actions conflict if they belong to different transactions, access the SAME data, and at least one is a <b>WRITE</b>.<br/>• Draw edge Ti → Tj if Ti executed conflicting action before Tj.<br/>• <b>Rule:</b> Schedule is Conflict Serializable <b>IF AND ONLY IF the graph has NO CYCLES</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: If the precedence graph has no cycles, doing a Topological Sort gives the equivalent serial schedule order!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Maximum B+ Tree Order\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> Block size = 512 bytes, Key size = 10 bytes, Block pointer = 6 bytes. Find maximum order p of an internal node.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Formula: p × 6 + (p - 1) × 10 ≤ 512.<br/>• Step 2: Expand: 6p + 10p - 10 ≤ 512 ⇒ 16p ≤ 522.<br/>• Step 3: Solve: p ≤ 522 / 16 = 32.625.<br/>• Step 4: Take floor integer: p = 32.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Maximum order p = 32.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Cascadeless vs Recoverable</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Strict ⊂ Cascadeless ⊂ Recoverable. A cascadeless schedule is ALWAYS recoverable, but a recoverable schedule is not necessarily cascadeless!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('CN', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: Mailing an Overseas Birthday Gift (Layering)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Application: You write a birthday greeting note.\n• Transport: Put a parcel tracking code on it.\n• Network: Write the destination Country and PIN code (IP Address routing).\n• Data Link: Truck driver reads the license plate of the next regional warehouse (MAC address).\n• Physical: The asphalt highway road.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: Wide River Pipe vs Fast Jet Speed (Bandwidth vs Latency)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Bandwidth is the width of a water pipe (how many liters flow per second). Propagation delay is how fast the water flows through the hose (speed of light). A huge cargo ship has massive bandwidth (can carry 1,000,000 hard drives), but high latency (takes 2 weeks). Playing online games needs low latency, not giant bandwidth.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Cars on a Long Highway (Bandwidth-Delay Product - BDP)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          If 100 cars enter a 500-km highway every minute, there are thousands of cars driving on the road at any given instant. Bandwidth-Delay Product (Bandwidth × Propagation Delay) measures how many data bits can fill the entire length of the cable pipe at once!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>32.1 Transmission Delay vs Propagation Delay</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Transmission Delay (T_trans):</b> Time to push packet onto wire = <b>Packet Size (L) / Bandwidth (B)</b>.<br/>• <b>Propagation Delay (T_prop):</b> Time for bit to travel across physical distance = <b>Distance (d) / Speed (v)</b>.<br/>• Total Latency = T_trans + T_prop + Queueing + Processing.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Bandwidth NEVER affects propagation speed! Propagation speed depends only on the speed of light in the cable.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>32.2 Store-and-Forward Packet Switching Delay</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          For M packets of size L traveling over N router hops with bandwidth B:<br/>• <b>Total Time = (N + M - 1) × (L / B) + N × T_prop</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Bandwidth is ALWAYS in bits per second (bps, Mbps). If packet size is given in Bytes, multiply by 8 first!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Packet Transmission Delay\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> A 1000-byte packet is sent over a 1 Mbps link. What is the transmission delay?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Convert bytes to bits: 1000 Bytes × 8 = 8000 bits.<br/>• Step 2: Bandwidth = 1 Mbps = 1,000,000 bits/sec.<br/>• Step 3: T_trans = 8000 / 1,000,000 = 0.008 seconds = 8 milliseconds.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Transmission Delay = 8 ms.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Bits vs Bytes</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Forgetting to multiply Bytes by 8 before dividing by Mbps will make your answer 8 times too small!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('CN', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: Talking at a Dinner Party (CSMA/CD)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          At a crowded dinner table, you listen before speaking (Carrier Sense). If someone is talking, you wait. If the room is quiet, you speak. If you and another person start speaking at the exact same instant, you collide! You both stop, yell \'Oops!\' (Jamming signal), and wait a random number of seconds (Backoff) before trying again.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: Walkie-Talkie vs Phone Call (Stop-and-Wait vs Sliding Window)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          In Stop-and-Wait, you say: \'Roger, over\' and wait 30 seconds for a reply. Channel sits idle! In a Sliding Window protocol (Go-Back-N), you send 7 packets in a continuous stream without waiting, sliding the window forward as ACKs return.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: The Tamper-Proof Seal (Cyclic Redundancy Check - CRC)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          CRC treats data bits like a polynomial and divides it by a generator code using binary XOR division. The remainder is attached to the packet. If even one bit flips during transit, the receiver\'s division fails, catching the error instantly.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>33.1 Sliding Window Window Sizes</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          With k sequence bits:<br/>• <b>Go-Back-N (GBN):</b> Sender window = <b>2^k - 1</b>, Receiver window = 1.<br/>• <b>Selective Repeat (SR):</b> Sender window = <b>2^(k-1)</b>, Receiver window = <b>2^(k-1)</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Stop-and-Wait efficiency η = 1 / (1 + 2a), where a = T_prop / T_trans.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>33.2 CSMA/CD Minimum Frame Size Rule</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          For Ethernet CSMA/CD to detect collisions before finishing transmission:<br/>• <b>T_trans ≥ 2 × T_prop</b>.<br/>• <b>Minimum Frame Size: L_min ≥ 2 × (d / v) × Bandwidth</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Remember the factor of 2! The collision signal has to travel all the way back across the cable: Round-Trip Time = 2 × T_prop.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Minimum Frame Size in CSMA/CD\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> A 10 Mbps Ethernet cable has propagation delay T_prop = 20 µs. What is the minimum frame size?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Formula: L_min = 2 × T_prop × Bandwidth.<br/>• Step 2: Plug in: L_min = 2 × (20 × 10^-6 s) × (10 × 10^6 bps).<br/>• Step 3: L_min = 40 × 10 = 400 bits.<br/>• Step 4: Convert to bytes: 400 / 8 = 50 Bytes.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Minimum frame size = 50 Bytes (400 bits).\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: CRC Check Bits</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          If the generator polynomial G(x) has degree r (e.g. x^3 + x + 1 has degree 3), append exactly r zeros (3 zeros), NOT 4 zeros!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('CN', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: Apartment Building Chutes (CIDR Subnetting)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          An IP block `192.168.1.0/24` has 256 addresses. The /24 tells the mail carrier that the first 24 bits are the building\'s street address (Network ID). The remaining 8 bits are the individual room numbers (Host ID).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: Moving a Huge Sofa Through a Narrow Door (IPv4 Fragmentation)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          If a delivery sofa is 8 feet long, but the doorway (MTU) only fits 3-foot boxes, you must unscrew the sofa into 3 numbered boxes with offset tags: Box 1 (Offset 0), Box 2 (Offset 185), Box 3 (Offset 370). In IPv4, Fragment Offset is measured in 8-byte blocks.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Whispering Rumors (Distance Vector Count-to-Infinity)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          In Distance Vector (RIP), routers whisper route distances to neighbors every 30 seconds. If a link breaks, routers keep updating each other in circles (2, 3, 4... up to 16!). Split Horizon and Poisoned Reverse stop these loops.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>34.1 IPv4 Fragmentation & Offset Rules</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Fragment Offset:</b> 13 bits. Measures byte offset in <b>units of 8 bytes</b>: `Offset = (Payload bytes) / 8`.<br/>• Fragment payload size MUST be a multiple of 8 (except the last fragment).<br/>• <b>MF bit (More Fragments):</b> 1 if more fragments follow; 0 for the last fragment.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Offset is ALWAYS divided by 8! If byte offset is 800, the offset field stores 800 / 8 = 100.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>34.2 Longest Prefix Match in Routing</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          When an IP matches multiple entries in a routing table, the router ALWAYS routes packet through the entry with the <b>LONGEST SUBNET MASK (most specific prefix)</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: If a packet matches both /16 and /24, route according to /24!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: IPv4 Usable Hosts\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> How many usable host IP addresses are in a /26 subnet?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Total bits = 32. Host bits h = 32 - 26 = 6 bits.<br/>• Step 2: Total IP addresses = 2^6 = 64.<br/>• Step 3: Subtract 2 addresses (Network ID and Direct Broadcast Address).<br/>• Step 4: Usable hosts = 64 - 2 = 62.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Usable hosts = 62.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: First and Last IP Addresses</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Always subtract 2 for usable hosts! The all-zeros host address is Network ID; the all-ones host address is Broadcast.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('CN', 3): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: The Three-Way Hello Handshake (TCP 3-Way Handshake)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Before two people talk on a phone: \'Can you hear me?\' (SYN) -> \'Yes, I hear you, can you hear me?\' (SYN+ACK) -> \'Yes, loud and clear!\' (ACK). Connection established! Now data can flow reliably.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: Squeezing a Water Hose (TCP Congestion Control)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          TCP starts sending slowly (Slow Start: doubles window every round). When it reaches a threshold, it increases gently by +1 packet per round (Additive Increase). If a packet drops (congestion!), it immediately cuts its speed in HALF (Multiplicative Decrease - AIMD).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Phone Contact List (DNS)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          You don\'t memorize 142.250.190.46; you type \'google.com\'. The Domain Name System (DNS) looks up the name like a global phonebook and returns the numerical IP address using fast, lightweight UDP (port 53).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>35.1 TCP Handshake & Sequence Numbers</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>3-Way Handshake:</b> SYN -> SYN+ACK -> ACK.<br/>• <b>Sequence numbers:</b> SYN and FIN packets <b>CONSUME 1 SEQUENCE NUMBER</b> even if they have 0 bytes of payload!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Plain ACK packets consume 0 sequence numbers.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>35.2 TCP Congestion Window Growth</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Slow Start:</b> Window doubles every RTT (1, 2, 4, 8...).<br/>• <b>Congestion Avoidance:</b> Increases by +1 MSS per RTT.<br/>• <b>On Timeout:</b> ssthresh = cwnd / 2, and <b>cwnd resets to 1 MSS</b>.<br/>• <b>On 3 Duplicate ACKs (TCP Reno):</b> cwnd drops to ssthresh (Fast Recovery, does not reset to 1!).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: Common Port Numbers to memorize: DNS (53), HTTP (80), HTTPS (443), SMTP (25), POP3 (110).\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: TCP Window Growth\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> TCP starts Slow Start with cwnd = 1 MSS, ssthresh = 8 MSS. How big is cwnd after 4 RTTs (no loss)?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Start: cwnd = 1.<br/>• Step 2: RTT 1: cwnd = 2.<br/>• Step 3: RTT 2: cwnd = 4.<br/>• Step 4: RTT 3: cwnd = 8 (Hits ssthresh = 8! Switches to Congestion Avoidance).<br/>• Step 5: RTT 4: Linear increase (+1) ⇒ cwnd = 8 + 1 = 9 MSS.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 After 4 RTTs, cwnd = 9 MSS.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: HTTP Statelessness</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          HTTP is stateless by design. Websites use Cookies and Session tokens to remember who you are.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('GA', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: The Misleading Group (Subject-Verb Agreement)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Sentence: \'The teacher, along with 50 students, (was/were) present\'. Many pick \'were\' because they see 50 students. But phrases with \'along with\' or \'as well as\' don\'t change the subject! The true subject is \'The teacher\' (singular), so the verb MUST be \'was\'.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: The Attacking Door (Dangling Modifier)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          A sign says: \'Walking into the bank, the door hit Alice\'. That means the door was walking! Correct: \'While Alice was walking into the bank, she was hit by the door\'.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Word Roots (Etymology)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          \'Bene\' means good (Benefit, Benevolent). \'Mal\' means bad (Malfunction, Malevolent). Knowing root prefixes lets you guess hundreds of unfamiliar words in 5 seconds.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>36.1 Subject-Verb & Either/Neither Rules</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • In \'Neither A nor B\' / \'Either A or B\': Verb agrees with the <b>SUBJECT CLOSEST TO IT</b>.<br/>• Words like \'Each\', \'Everyone\', \'Neither of\' are STRICTLY SINGULAR (\'Each of the boys <b>is</b> ready\').\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: \'Complement\' means something that completes (Wine complements cheese). \'Compliment\' means praise (She gave a nice compliment).\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Sentence Correction\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> Select the correct sentence:\n(A) Neither the teacher nor the students was present.\n(B) Neither the teacher nor the students were present.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Check subject closest to the verb: \'students\' (plural).<br/>• Step 2: Plural subject requires plural verb \'were\'.<br/>• Step 3: Option (B) is grammatically correct.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Sentence (B) is correct.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Third Conditional Form</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Formula: \'If + had + V3, would have + V3\'. Example: \'If he had studied, he would have passed\' (Never write \'If he would have studied\'!).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('GA', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: The Escalator Run (Relative Speed)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          An escalator moves up at 1 m/s. If you stand still, you take 60 seconds. If you run up at 2 m/s, your speeds add: (2 + 1) = 3 m/s, taking just 20 seconds! Moving in same direction adds speeds; moving against subtracts.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: Painting a Room Together (Time and Work)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Alice paints a room in 10 days (1/10th per day). Bob paints it in 15 days (1/15th per day). Working together, daily work = 1/10 + 1/15 = 5/30 = 1/6th per day. Together they finish the whole room in 6 days!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Diwali Sale 50% + 50% Off! (Successive Percentages)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Does 50% + 50% off mean clothes are free? No! ₹1000 shirt - 50% becomes ₹500. The second 50% off ₹500 makes it ₹250. Real discount is 75%, not 100%! Formula: Net = a + b + (ab/100).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>37.1 Key Arithmetic Formulas to Memorize</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • <b>Average Speed for equal distances:</b> <b>2 v1 v2 / (v1 + v2)</b> (Harmonic mean!).<br/>• <b>Difference between CI and SI for 2 years:</b> <b>P × (R / 100)²</b>.<br/>• <b>Boats & Streams:</b> Downstream = u + v &nbsp;|&nbsp; Upstream = u - v.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: If price rises by R%, consumption must drop by [ R / (100 + R) ] × 100% to keep spending unchanged.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Time and Work Alternate Days\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> A completes work in 10 days, B in 15 days. They work alternate days starting with A. How many days will it take?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Total work LCM(10, 15) = 30 units.<br/>• Step 2: A does 30/10 = 3 units/day. B does 30/15 = 2 units/day.<br/>• Step 3: In 2 days (A + B), work done = 3 + 2 = 5 units.<br/>• Step 4: Cycles needed: 30 / 5 = 6 cycles.<br/>• Step 5: Total days = 6 cycles × 2 days = 12 days.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Total time = 12 days.\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: Average Speed is NOT Simple Average</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          If distance is equal, Average Speed is 2v1v2 / (v1 + v2), NOT (v1 + v2)/2!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('GA', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS) -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. EVERYDAY REAL-LIFE EXAMPLES (INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 italic mt-0.5">\n        These everyday real-life examples illustrate the core concept using familiar situations before diving into theory:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 1: Folding Paper & Punching a Hole (Spatial Aptitude)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          Fold a paper in half, then half again, and punch 1 hole. When you unfold it, the crease lines act as mirrors. That 1 punched hole reflects symmetrically into 4 holes in the opened paper!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 2: Folding a Cardboard Cube (Cube Nets)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          In any straight strip of squares on a flat cube net, alternate squares (separated by 1 square in between) will ALWAYS end up on opposite sides when folded into a 3D box!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Real-Life Analogy</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Example 3: Blood Relations Clue Tree\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          \'His mother is the only daughter of my mother\'. Start from \'My mother\': Only daughter of my mother is my sister. \'His mother is my sister\'. So the boy is my nephew! Always draw a simple tree diagram.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. IN-DEPTH CONCEPTUAL & ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/60 dark:bg-slate-800/40 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/60 space-y-2">\n        <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>38.1 Syllogisms & Validity Rules</span>\n        </h3>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">\n          • A conclusion is valid ONLY if it holds in <b>EVERY POSSIBLE Venn diagram</b>.<br/>• \'Some\' in logic means \'At least one, and possibly all\'.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold">\n          GOLDEN RULE: In a folded 3D cube, two OPPOSITE faces can NEVER touch or appear adjacent to each other!\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Cube Net Opposite Faces\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem:</b> In a vertical cube net strip [1, 2, 3, 4], which faces are opposite?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Alternate faces in a straight line are opposite.<br/>• Step 2: Face 1 is opposite to Face 3.<br/>• Step 3: Face 2 is opposite to Face 4.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold">\n          🎯 Opposite pairs: (1, 3) and (2, 4).\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50/80 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200/80 dark:border-rose-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-rose-900 dark:text-rose-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Trap: \'Some\' vs \'All\'</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          If \'All dogs are animals\' is true, then \'Some dogs are animals\' is also mathematically 100% TRUE!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>'}
